#: ../../ref-manual/buildtools-alma-centos-rocky-9.rst:3
msgid ""
"You will also need to use a :term:`buildtools` tarball which provides a "
"set of basic host tools with fixed versions. Follow the :ref:`system-"
"requirements-buildtools` section below to learn how to download and "
"install this tarball."
msgstr "Sarà inoltre necessario utilizzare un file tarball :term:`buildtools` che fornisce una serie di strumenti host di base con versioni fisse. Seguire la sezione :ref:`system-requirements-buildtools` di seguito per scoprire come scaricare e installare questo file tarball."

#: ../../ref-manual/buildtools-alma-centos-rocky-9.rst:9
msgid ""
"The AlmaLinux 9, CentOS 9 and RockyLinux 9 distributions are affected by "
"a bug described in :yocto_bugs:`/show_bug.cgi?id=16143`, which is the "
"reason why a :term:`buildtools` tarball is required."
msgstr "Le distribuzioni AlmaLinux 9, CentOS 9 e RockyLinux 9 sono interessate da un bug descritto in :yocto_bugs:`/show_bug.cgi?id=16143`, motivo per cui è necessario un file tarball :term:`buildtools`."

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:5
msgid "Classes"
msgstr "Classi"

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:7
msgid ""
"Class files are used to abstract common functionality and share it "
"amongst multiple recipe (``.bb``) files. To use a class file, you simply "
"make sure the recipe inherits the class. In most cases, when a recipe "
"inherits a class it is enough to enable its features. There are cases, "
"however, where in the recipe you might need to set variables or override "
"some default behavior. A class can also be inherited globally (in every "
"recipe) with the :term:`INHERIT` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:15
msgid ""
"Any :term:`Metadata` usually found in a recipe can also be placed in a "
"class file. Class files are identified by the extension ``.bbclass`` and "
"are usually placed in one of the following subdirectories of a "
":term:`layer`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:20
msgid ""
"``classes-recipe/`` - classes intended to be inherited by recipes "
"individually"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:22
msgid "``classes-global/`` - classes intended to be inherited globally"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:23
msgid "``classes/`` - classes whose usage context is not clearly defined"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:25
msgid ""
"Class files can also be pointed to by :term:`BUILDDIR` (e.g. ``build/``) "
"in the same way as ``.conf`` files in the ``conf`` directory. Class files"
" are searched for in :term:`BBPATH` using the same method by which "
"``.conf`` files are searched."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:31
msgid ""
"This chapter discusses only the most useful and important classes. Other "
"classes do exist within the ``meta/classes*`` directories of :term"
":`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)`. You can refer to the ``.bbclass`` files "
"directly for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:39
msgid "``allarch``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:41
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-allarch` class is inherited by recipes that do not "
"produce architecture-specific output. The class disables functionality "
"that is normally needed for recipes that produce executable binaries "
"(such as building the cross-compiler and a C library as pre-requisites, "
"and splitting out of debug symbols during packaging)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:49
msgid ""
"Unlike some distro recipes (e.g. Debian), OpenEmbedded recipes that "
"produce packages that depend on tunings through use of the "
":term:`RDEPENDS` and :term:`TUNE_PKGARCH` variables, should never be "
"configured for all architectures using :ref:`ref-classes-allarch`. This "
"is the case even if the recipes do not produce architecture-specific "
"output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:56
msgid ""
"Configuring such recipes for all architectures causes the "
":ref:`do_package_write_* <ref-tasks-package_write_deb>` tasks to have "
"different signatures for the machines with different tunings. "
"Additionally, unnecessary rebuilds occur every time an image for a "
"different :term:`MACHINE` is built even when the recipe never changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:62
msgid ""
"By default, all recipes inherit the :ref:`ref-classes-base` and :ref"
":`ref-classes-package` classes, which enable functionality needed for "
"recipes that produce executable output. If your recipe, for example, only"
" produces packages that contain configuration files, media files, or "
"scripts (e.g. Python and Perl), then it should inherit the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-allarch` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:72
msgid "``archiver``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:74
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-archiver` class supports releasing source code and "
"other materials with the binaries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:77
msgid ""
"For more details on the source :ref:`ref-classes-archiver`, see the "
"\":ref:`dev-manual/licenses:maintaining open source license compliance "
"during your product's lifecycle`\" section in the Yocto Project "
"Development Tasks Manual. You can also see the :term:`ARCHIVER_MODE` "
"variable for information about the variable flags (varflags) that help "
"control archive creation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:86
msgid "``autotools*``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:88
msgid ""
"The :ref:`autotools* <ref-classes-autotools>` classes support packages "
"built with the :wikipedia:`GNU Autotools <GNU_Autotools>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:91
msgid ""
"The ``autoconf``, ``automake``, and ``libtool`` packages bring "
"standardization. This class defines a set of tasks (e.g. ``configure``, "
"``compile`` and so forth) that work for all Autotooled packages. It "
"should usually be enough to define a few standard variables and then "
"simply ``inherit autotools``. These classes can also work with software "
"that emulates Autotools. For more information, see the \":ref:`dev-manual"
"/new-recipe:building an autotooled package`\" section in the Yocto "
"Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:100
msgid ""
"By default, the :ref:`autotools* <ref-classes-autotools>` classes use "
"out-of-tree builds (i.e. ``autotools.bbclass`` building with ``B != S``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:103
msgid ""
"If the software being built by a recipe does not support using out-of-"
"tree builds, you should have the recipe inherit the :ref:`autotools-"
"brokensep <ref-classes-autotools>` class. The :ref:`autotools-brokensep "
"<ref-classes-autotools>` class behaves the same as the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"autotools` class but builds with :term:`B` == :term:`S`. This method is "
"useful when out-of-tree build support is either not present or is broken."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:112
msgid ""
"It is recommended that out-of-tree support be fixed and used if at all "
"possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:115
msgid ""
"It's useful to have some idea of how the tasks defined by the "
":ref:`autotools* <ref-classes-autotools>` classes work and what they do "
"behind the scenes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:118
msgid ""
":ref:`ref-tasks-configure` --- regenerates the configure script (using "
"``autoreconf``) and then launches it with a standard set of arguments "
"used during cross-compilation. You can pass additional parameters to "
"``configure`` through the :term:`EXTRA_OECONF` or "
":term:`PACKAGECONFIG_CONFARGS` variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:125
msgid ""
":ref:`ref-tasks-compile` --- runs ``make`` with arguments that specify "
"the compiler and linker. You can pass additional arguments through the "
":term:`EXTRA_OEMAKE` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:129
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
":ref:`ref-tasks-install` --- runs ``make install`` and passes in ``${``\\"
" :term:`D`\\ ``}`` as ``DESTDIR``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:135
msgid "``barebox``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:137
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-barebox` class manages building the barebox "
"bootloader."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:139
msgid ""
"If a file named ``defconfig`` is included in the :term:`SRC_URI`, it will"
" be copied to ``.config`` in the build directory and used as the barebox "
"configuration. Instead of providing a ``defconfig`` file, you can set "
":term:`BAREBOX_CONFIG` to a defconfig provided by the barebox source "
"tree. If neither ``defconfig`` nor :term:`BAREBOX_CONFIG` is specified, "
"the class will raise an error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:147
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-barebox` class supports config fragments and "
"internally includes the :ref:`ref-classes-cml1` class to provide `Kconfig"
" <https://docs.kernel.org/kbuild/kconfig-language.html>`__ support for "
"barebox, enabling tasks such as :ref:`ref-tasks-menuconfig` and :ref"
":`ref-tasks-diffconfig`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:153
msgid ""
"The generated barebox binaries are deployed to :term:`DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE` "
"as well as installed to ``BAREBOX_INSTALL_PATH`` (``/boot`` by default) "
"making them part of the recipe's base package. This setup supports both "
"using the barebox binaries as independent artifacts and installing them "
"into a rootfs. :term:`BAREBOX_BINARY` can be used to select a distinct "
"binary to deploy and install. If ``barebox`` is set as the "
":term:`EFI_PROVIDER`, the class will leverage :oe_git:`conf/image-"
"uefi.conf </openembedded-core/tree/meta/conf/image-uefi.conf>` to define "
"the default installation paths and naming conventions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:164
msgid ""
"The compiled-in barebox environment can be extended by adding environment"
" files to the ``BAREBOX_ENV_DIR``. The ``BAREBOX_FIRMWARE_DIR`` variable "
"allows you to specify the firmware blob search directory, enabling "
"loading of additional firmware like TF-A or OP-TEE."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:172
msgid "``base``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:174
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-base` class is special in that every ``.bb`` file "
"implicitly inherits the class. This class contains definitions for "
"standard basic tasks such as fetching, unpacking, configuring (empty by "
"default), compiling (runs any ``Makefile`` present), installing (empty by"
" default) and packaging (empty by default). These tasks are often "
"overridden or extended by other classes such as the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"autotools` class or the :ref:`ref-classes-package` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:182
msgid ""
"The class also contains some commonly used functions such as "
"``oe_runmake``, which runs ``make`` with the arguments specified in "
":term:`EXTRA_OEMAKE` variable as well as the arguments passed directly to"
" ``oe_runmake``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:190
msgid "``bash-completion``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:192
msgid ""
"Sets up packaging and dependencies appropriate for recipes that build "
"software that includes bash-completion data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:198
msgid "``bin_package``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:200
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-bin-package` class is a helper class for recipes, "
"that disables the :ref:`ref-tasks-configure` and :ref:`ref-tasks-compile`"
" tasks and copies the content of the :term:`S` directory into the "
":term:`D` directory. This is useful for installing binary packages (e.g. "
"RPM packages) by passing the package in the :term:`SRC_URI` variable and "
"inheriting this class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:206
msgid ""
"For RPMs and other packages that do not contain a subdirectory, you "
"should set the :term:`SRC_URI` option ``subdir`` to :term:`BP` so that "
"the contents are extracted to the directory expected by the default value"
" of :term:`S`. For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:213
msgid "This class can also be used for tarballs. For example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:217
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-bin-package` class will copy the extracted content "
"of the tarball from :term:`S` to :term:`D`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:220
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"This class assumes that the content of the package as installed in "
":term:`S` mirrors the expected layout once installed on the target, which"
" is generally the case for binary packages. For example, an RPM package "
"for a library would usually contain the ``usr/lib`` directory, and should"
" be extracted to ``${S}/usr/lib/<library>.so.<version>`` to be installed "
"in :term:`D` correctly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:228
msgid ""
"The extraction of the package passed in :term:`SRC_URI` is not handled by"
" the :ref:`ref-classes-bin-package` class, but rather by the appropriate "
":ref:`fetcher <bitbake-user-manual/bitbake-user-manual-"
"fetching:fetchers>` depending on the file extension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:236
msgid "``binconfig``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:238
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-binconfig` class helps to correct paths in shell "
"scripts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:240
msgid ""
"Before ``pkg-config`` had become widespread, libraries shipped shell "
"scripts to give information about the libraries and include paths needed "
"to build software (usually named ``LIBNAME-config``). This class assists "
"any recipe using such scripts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:245
msgid ""
"During staging, the OpenEmbedded build system installs such scripts into "
"the ``sysroots/`` directory. Inheriting this class results in all paths "
"in these scripts being changed to point into the ``sysroots/`` directory "
"so that all builds that use the script use the correct directories for "
"the cross compiling layout. See the :term:`BINCONFIG_GLOB` variable for "
"more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:256
msgid "``binconfig-disabled``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:258
msgid ""
"An alternative version of the :ref:`ref-classes-binconfig` class, which "
"disables binary configuration scripts by making them return an error in "
"favor of using ``pkg-config`` to query the information. The scripts to be"
" disabled should be specified using the :term:`BINCONFIG` variable within"
" the recipe inheriting the class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:267
msgid "``buildhistory``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:269
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-buildhistory` class records a history of build "
"output metadata, which can be used to detect possible regressions as well"
" as used for analysis of the build output. For more information on using "
"Build History, see the \":ref:`dev-manual/build-quality:maintaining build"
" output quality with \\`\\`buildhistory\\`\\``\" section in the Yocto "
"Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:279
msgid "``buildstats``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:281
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-buildstats` class records performance statistics "
"about each task executed during the build (e.g. elapsed time, CPU usage, "
"and I/O usage)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:284
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"When you use this class, the output goes into the :term:`BUILDSTATS_BASE`"
" directory, which defaults to ``${TMPDIR}/buildstats/``. You can analyze "
"the elapsed time using ``scripts/pybootchartgui/pybootchartgui.py``, "
"which produces a cascading chart of the entire build process and can be "
"useful for highlighting bottlenecks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:291
msgid ""
"If you want to enable the class, you can add \":ref:`ref-classes-"
"buildstats`\" to the :term:`USER_CLASSES` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:297
msgid "``buildstats-summary``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:299
msgid ""
"When inherited globally, prints statistics at the end of the build on "
"sstate re-use. In order to function, this class requires the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-buildstats` class be enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:306
msgid "``cargo``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:308
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cargo` class allows to compile Rust language "
"programs using `Cargo <https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/>`__. Cargo is "
"Rust's package manager, allowing to fetch package dependencies and build "
"your program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:312
msgid ""
"Using this class makes it very easy to build Rust programs. All you need "
"is to use the :term:`SRC_URI` variable to point to a source repository "
"which can be built by Cargo, typically one that was created by the "
"``cargo new`` command, containing a ``Cargo.toml`` file, a ``Cargo.lock``"
" file and a ``src`` subdirectory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:318
msgid ""
"If you want to build and package tests of the program, inherit the :ref"
":`ref-classes-ptest-cargo` class instead of :ref:`ref-classes-cargo`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:321
msgid ""
"You will find an example (that show also how to handle possible git "
"source dependencies) in the :oe_git:`zvariant_3.12.0.bb </openembedded-"
"core/tree/meta-selftest/recipes-extended/zvariant/zvariant_3.12.0.bb>` "
"recipe. Another example, with only crate dependencies, is the :oe_git"
":`uutils-coreutils </meta-openembedded/tree/meta-oe/recipes-core/uutils-"
"coreutils>` recipe, which was generated by the `cargo-bitbake "
"<https://crates.io/crates/cargo-bitbake>`__ tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:328
msgid "This class inherits the :ref:`ref-classes-cargo_common` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:333
msgid "``cargo_c``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:335
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cargo_c` class can be inherited by a recipe to "
"generate a Rust library that can be called by C/C++ code. The recipe "
"which inherits this class has to only replace ``inherit cargo`` by "
"``inherit cargo_c``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:339
msgid ""
"See the :oe_git:`rust-c-lib-example_git.bb </openembedded-core/tree/meta-"
"selftest/recipes-devtools/rust/rust-c-lib-example_git.bb>` example "
"recipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:346
msgid "``cargo_common``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:348
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cargo_common` class is an internal class that is "
"not intended to be used directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:351
msgid ""
"An exception is the \"rust\" recipe, to build the Rust compiler and "
"runtime library, which is built by Cargo but cannot use the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-cargo` class. This is why this class was introduced."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:358
msgid "``cargo-update-recipe-crates``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:360
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cargo-update-recipe-crates` class allows recipe "
"developers to update the list of Cargo crates in :term:`SRC_URI` by "
"reading the ``Cargo.lock`` file in the source tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:364
msgid ""
"To do so, create a recipe for your program, for example using "
":doc:`devtool </ref-manual/devtool-reference>`, make it inherit the :ref"
":`ref-classes-cargo` and :ref:`ref-classes-cargo-update-recipe-crates` "
"and run::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:371
msgid ""
"This creates a ``recipe-crates.inc`` file that you can include in your "
"recipe::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:376
msgid ""
"That's also something you can achieve by using the `cargo-bitbake "
"<https://crates.io/crates/cargo-bitbake>`__ tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:382
msgid "``ccache``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:384
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-ccache` class enables the C/C++ Compiler Cache for "
"the build. This class is used to give a minor performance boost during "
"the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:387
msgid ""
"See https://ccache.samba.org/ for information on the C/C++ Compiler "
"Cache, and the :oe_git:`ccache.bbclass </openembedded-"
"core/tree/meta/classes/ccache.bbclass>` file for details about how to "
"enable this mechanism in your configuration file, how to disable it for "
"specific recipes, and how to share ``ccache`` files between builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:393
msgid ""
"Recipes (including :ref:`ref-classes-native` ones) can make use of the "
"host's ``ccache`` binary (via :term:`HOSTTOOLS`) if the following "
"configuration statements are provided in a :term:`configuration file`::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:400
msgid ""
"Recipes can also explicitly disable `Ccache` support even when the :ref"
":`ref-classes-ccache` class is enabled, by setting the "
":term:`CCACHE_DISABLE` variable to \"1\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:404
msgid ""
"Using the :ref:`ref-classes-ccache` class can lead to unexpected side-"
"effects. Using this class is not recommended."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:410
msgid "``chrpath``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:412
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-chrpath` class is a wrapper around the \"chrpath\" "
"utility, which is used during the build process for :ref:`ref-classes-"
"nativesdk`, :ref:`ref-classes-cross`, and :ref:`ref-classes-cross-"
"canadian` recipes to change ``RPATH`` records within binaries in order to"
" make them relocatable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:420
msgid "``cmake``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:422
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cmake` class allows recipes to build software using"
" the `CMake <https://cmake.org/overview/>`__ build system. You can use "
"the :term:`EXTRA_OECMAKE` variable to specify additional configuration "
"options to pass to the ``cmake`` command line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:427
msgid ""
"By default, the :ref:`ref-classes-cmake` class uses `Ninja <https"
"://ninja-build.org/>`__ instead of GNU make for building, which offers "
"better build performance. If a recipe is broken with Ninja, then the "
"recipe can set the :term:`OECMAKE_GENERATOR` variable to ``Unix "
"Makefiles`` to use GNU make instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:433
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"If you need to install custom CMake toolchain files supplied by the "
"application being built, you should install them (during :ref:`ref-tasks-"
"install`) to the preferred CMake Module directory: "
"``${D}${datadir}/cmake/modules/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:440
msgid "``cmake-qemu``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:442
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cmake-qemu` class might be used instead of the :ref"
":`ref-classes-cmake` class. In addition to the features provided by the "
":ref:`ref-classes-cmake` class, the :ref:`ref-classes-cmake-qemu` class "
"passes the ``CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING_EMULATOR`` setting to ``cmake``. This "
"allows to use QEMU user-mode emulation for the execution of cross-"
"compiled binaries on the host machine.  For more information about "
"``CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING_EMULATOR`` please refer to the `related section of"
" the CMake documentation "
"<https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/variable/CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING_EMULATOR.html>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:451
msgid ""
"Not all platforms are supported by QEMU. This class only works for "
"machines with ``qemu-usermode`` in the :ref:`ref-features-machine`. Using"
" QEMU user-mode therefore involves a certain risk, which is also the "
"reason why this feature is not part of the main :ref:`ref-classes-cmake` "
"class by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:456
msgid ""
"One use case is the execution of cross-compiled unit tests with CTest on "
"the build machine. If ``CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING_EMULATOR`` is configured::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:461
msgid "works transparently with QEMU user-mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:463
msgid ""
"If the CMake project is developed with this use case in mind this works "
"very nicely. This also applies to an IDE configured to use ``cmake-"
"native`` for cross-compiling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:469
msgid "``cml1``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:471
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cml1` class provides basic support for the Linux "
"kernel style build configuration system. \"cml\" stands for "
"\"Configuration Menu Language\", which originates from the Linux kernel "
"but is also used in other projects such as U-Boot and BusyBox. It could "
"have been called \"kconfig\" too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:476
msgid ""
"The CML configuration file location (``.config``) is by default expected "
"to be in the build directory (:term:`B`). This can be overridden with the"
" :term:`KCONFIG_CONFIG_ROOTDIR` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:483
msgid "``compress_doc``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:485
msgid ""
"Enables compression for manual and info pages. This class is intended to "
"be inherited globally. The default compression mechanism is gz (gzip) but"
" you can select an alternative mechanism by setting the "
":term:`DOC_COMPRESS` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:493
msgid "``copyleft_compliance``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:495
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-copyleft_compliance` class preserves source code "
"for the purposes of license compliance. This class is an alternative to "
"the :ref:`ref-classes-archiver` class and is still used by some users "
"even though it has been deprecated in favor of the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"archiver` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:503
msgid "``copyleft_filter``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:505
msgid ""
"A class used by the :ref:`ref-classes-archiver` and :ref:`ref-classes-"
"copyleft_compliance` classes for filtering licenses. The "
"``copyleft_filter`` class is an internal class and is not intended to be "
"used directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:513
msgid "``core-image``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:515
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-core-image` class provides common definitions for "
"the ``core-image-*`` image recipes, such as support for additional "
":term:`IMAGE_FEATURES`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:522
msgid "``cpan*``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:524
msgid "The :ref:`cpan* <ref-classes-cpan>` classes support Perl modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:526
msgid ""
"Recipes for Perl modules are simple. These recipes usually only need to "
"point to the source's archive and then inherit the proper class file. "
"Building is split into two methods depending on which method the module "
"authors used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:531
msgid ""
"Modules that use old ``Makefile.PL``-based build system require "
"``cpan.bbclass`` in their recipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:534
msgid ""
"Modules that use ``Build.PL``-based build system require using "
"``cpan_build.bbclass`` in their recipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:537
msgid ""
"Both build methods inherit the :ref:`cpan-base <ref-classes-cpan>` class "
"for basic Perl support."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:543
msgid "``create-spdx``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:545
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-create-spdx` class provides support for "
"automatically creating :term:`SPDX` :term:`SBOM` documents based upon "
"image and SDK contents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:549
msgid "This class is meant to be inherited globally from a configuration file::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:553
msgid ""
"The toplevel :term:`SPDX` output file is generated in JSON format as a "
"``IMAGE-MACHINE.spdx.json`` file in ``tmp/deploy/images/MACHINE/`` inside"
" the :term:`Build Directory`. There are other related files in the same "
"directory, as well as in ``tmp/deploy/spdx``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:558
msgid ""
"The exact behaviour of this class, and the amount of output can be "
"controlled by the :term:`SPDX_PRETTY`, :term:`SPDX_INCLUDE_SOURCES` and "
"other variables starting with ``SPDX_``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:562
msgid ""
"See the description of these variables and the \":ref:`dev-"
"manual/sbom:creating a software bill of materials`\" section in the Yocto"
" Project Development Tasks Manual for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:569
msgid "``cross``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:571
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cross` class provides support for the recipes that "
"build the cross-compilation tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:577
msgid "``cross-canadian``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:579
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cross-canadian` class provides support for the "
"recipes that build the Canadian Cross-compilation tools for SDKs. See the"
" \":ref:`overview-manual/concepts:cross-development toolchain "
"generation`\" section in the Yocto Project Overview and Concepts Manual "
"for more discussion on these cross-compilation tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:588
msgid "``crosssdk``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:590
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-crosssdk` class provides support for the recipes "
"that build the cross-compilation tools used for building SDKs. See the "
"\":ref:`overview-manual/concepts:cross-development toolchain "
"generation`\" section in the Yocto Project Overview and Concepts Manual "
"for more discussion on these cross-compilation tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:599
msgid "``cython``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:601
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-cython` class can be used by Python recipes that "
"require `Cython <https://cython.org/>`__ as part of their build "
"dependencies (:term:`DEPENDS`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:608
msgid "``debian``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:610
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-debian` class renames output packages so that they "
"follow the Debian naming policy (i.e. ``glibc`` becomes ``libc6`` and "
"``glibc-devel`` becomes ``libc6-dev``.) Renaming includes the library "
"name and version as part of the package name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:615
msgid ""
"If a recipe creates packages for multiple libraries (shared object files "
"of ``.so`` type), use the :term:`LEAD_SONAME` variable in the recipe to "
"specify the library on which to apply the naming scheme."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:623
msgid "``deploy``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:625
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-deploy` class handles deploying files to the "
":term:`DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE` directory. The main function of this class is to"
" allow the deploy step to be accelerated by shared state. Recipes that "
"inherit this class should define their own :ref:`ref-tasks-deploy` "
"function to copy the files to be deployed to :term:`DEPLOYDIR`, and use "
"``addtask`` to add the task at the appropriate place, which is usually "
"after :ref:`ref-tasks-compile` or :ref:`ref-tasks-install`. The class "
"then takes care of staging the files from :term:`DEPLOYDIR` to "
":term:`DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:639
msgid "``devicetree``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:641
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-devicetree` class allows to build a recipe that "
"compiles device tree source files that are not in the kernel tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:644
msgid ""
"The compilation of out-of-tree device tree sources is the same as the "
"kernel in-tree device tree compilation process. This includes the ability"
" to include sources from the kernel such as SoC ``dtsi`` files as well as"
" C header files, such as ``gpio.h``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:649
msgid "The :ref:`ref-tasks-compile` task will compile two kinds of files:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:651
msgid "Regular device tree sources with a ``.dts`` extension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:653
msgid ""
"Device tree overlays, detected from the presence of the ``/plugin/;`` "
"string in the file contents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:656
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"This class deploys the generated device tree binaries into ``${``\\ "
":term:`DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE`\\ ``}/devicetree/``.  This is similar to what "
"the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-devicetree` class does, with the added "
"``devicetree`` subdirectory to avoid name clashes. Additionally, the "
"device trees are populated into the sysroot for access via the sysroot "
"from within other recipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:663
msgid ""
"By default, all device tree sources located in :term:`DT_FILES_PATH` "
"directory are compiled. To select only particular sources, set "
":term:`DT_FILES` to a space-separated list of files (relative to "
":term:`DT_FILES_PATH`). For convenience, both ``.dts`` and ``.dtb`` "
"extensions can be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:668
msgid ""
"An extra padding is appended to non-overlay device trees binaries. This "
"can typically be used as extra space for adding extra properties at boot "
"time. The padding size can be modified by setting :term:`DT_PADDING_SIZE`"
" to the desired size, in bytes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:673
msgid ""
"See :oe_git:`devicetree.bbclass sources </openembedded-core/tree/meta"
"/classes-recipe/devicetree.bbclass>` for further variables controlling "
"this class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:677
msgid ""
"Here is an excerpt of an example ``recipes-kernel/linux/devicetree-"
"acme.bb`` recipe inheriting this class::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:687
msgid "``devshell``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:689
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-devshell` class adds the :ref:`ref-tasks-devshell` "
"task. Distribution policy dictates whether to include this class. See the"
" \":ref:`dev-manual/development-shell:using a development shell`\" "
"section in the Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual for more "
"information about using :ref:`ref-classes-devshell`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:697
msgid "``devupstream``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:699
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-devupstream` class uses :term:`BBCLASSEXTEND` to "
"add a variant of the recipe that fetches from an alternative URI (e.g. "
"Git) instead of a tarball. Here is an example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:708
msgid ""
"Adding the above statements to your recipe creates a variant that has "
":term:`DEFAULT_PREFERENCE` set to \"-1\". Consequently, you need to "
"select the variant of the recipe to use it. Any development-specific "
"adjustments can be done by using the ``class-devupstream`` override. Here"
" is an example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:719
msgid ""
"The class currently only supports creating a development variant of the "
"target recipe, not :ref:`ref-classes-native` or :ref:`ref-classes-"
"nativesdk` variants."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:723
msgid ""
"The :term:`BBCLASSEXTEND` syntax (i.e. ``devupstream:target``) provides "
"support for :ref:`ref-classes-native` and :ref:`ref-classes-nativesdk` "
"variants. Consequently, this functionality can be added in a future "
"release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:727
msgid ""
"Support for other version control systems such as Subversion is limited "
"due to BitBake's automatic fetch dependencies (e.g. ``subversion-"
"native``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:734
msgid "``externalsrc``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:736
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-externalsrc` class supports building software from "
"source code that is external to the OpenEmbedded build system. Building "
"software from an external source tree means that the build system's "
"normal fetch, unpack, and patch process is not used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:741
msgid ""
"By default, the OpenEmbedded build system uses the :term:`S` and "
":term:`B` variables to locate unpacked recipe source code and to build "
"it, respectively. When your recipe inherits the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"externalsrc` class, you use the :term:`EXTERNALSRC` and "
":term:`EXTERNALSRC_BUILD` variables to ultimately define :term:`S` and "
":term:`B`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:748
msgid ""
"By default, this class expects the source code to support recipe builds "
"that use the :term:`B` variable to point to the directory in which the "
"OpenEmbedded build system places the generated objects built from the "
"recipes. By default, the :term:`B` directory is set to the following, "
"which is separate from the source directory (:term:`S`)::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:756
msgid ""
"See these variables for more information: :term:`WORKDIR`, :term:`BPN`, "
"and :term:`PV`,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:760
msgid ""
"For more information on the :ref:`ref-classes-externalsrc` class, see the"
" comments in ``meta/classes/externalsrc.bbclass`` in :term:`OpenEmbedded-"
"Core (OE-Core)`. For information on how to use the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"externalsrc` class, see the \":ref:`dev-manual/building:building software"
" from an external source`\" section in the Yocto Project Development "
"Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:769
msgid "``extrausers``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:771
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-extrausers` class allows additional user and group "
"configuration to be applied at the image level. Inheriting this class "
"either globally or from an image recipe allows additional user and group "
"operations to be performed using the :term:`EXTRA_USERS_PARAMS` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:779
msgid ""
"The user and group operations added using the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"extrausers` class are not tied to a specific recipe outside of the recipe"
" for the image. Thus, the operations can be performed across the image as"
" a whole. Use the :ref:`ref-classes-useradd` class to add user and group "
"configuration to a specific recipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:785
msgid "Here is an example that uses this class in an image recipe::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:797 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3048
msgid ""
"Here is an example that adds two users named \"tester-jim\" and \"tester-"
"sue\" and assigns passwords. First on host, create the (escaped) password"
" hash::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:802 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3053
msgid ""
"The resulting hash is set to a variable and used in ``useradd`` command "
"parameters::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:811 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3062
msgid "Finally, here is an example that sets the root password::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:820 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3071
msgid ""
"From a security perspective, hardcoding a default password is not "
"generally a good idea or even legal in some jurisdictions. It is "
"recommended that you do not do this if you are building a production "
"image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:829
msgid "``features_check``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:831
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-features_check` class allows individual recipes to "
"check for required and conflicting :term:`DISTRO_FEATURES`, "
":term:`MACHINE_FEATURES` or :term:`COMBINED_FEATURES`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:835
msgid "This class provides support for the following variables:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:837 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:8532
msgid ":term:`REQUIRED_DISTRO_FEATURES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:838 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1826
msgid ":term:`CONFLICT_DISTRO_FEATURES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:839 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:133
msgid ":term:`ANY_OF_DISTRO_FEATURES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:840
msgid "``REQUIRED_MACHINE_FEATURES``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:841
msgid "``CONFLICT_MACHINE_FEATURES``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:842
msgid "``ANY_OF_MACHINE_FEATURES``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:843
msgid "``REQUIRED_COMBINED_FEATURES``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:844
msgid "``CONFLICT_COMBINED_FEATURES``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:845
msgid "``ANY_OF_COMBINED_FEATURES``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:847
msgid ""
"If any conditions specified in the recipe using the above variables are "
"not met, the recipe will be skipped, and if the build system attempts to "
"build the recipe then an error will be triggered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:855
msgid "``fontcache``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:857
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-fontcache` class generates the proper post-install "
"and post-remove (postinst and postrm) scriptlets for font packages. These"
" scriptlets call ``fc-cache`` (part of ``Fontconfig``) to add the fonts "
"to the font information cache. Since the cache files are architecture-"
"specific, ``fc-cache`` runs using QEMU if the postinst scriptlets need to"
" be run on the build host during image creation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:864
msgid ""
"If the fonts being installed are in packages other than the main package,"
" set :term:`FONT_PACKAGES` to specify the packages containing the fonts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:871
msgid "``fs-uuid``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:873
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-fs-uuid` class extracts UUID from ``${``\\ "
":term:`ROOTFS`\\ ``}``, which must have been built by the time that this "
"function gets called. The :ref:`ref-classes-fs-uuid` class only works on "
"``ext`` file systems and depends on ``tune2fs``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:881
msgid "``gconf``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:883
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-gconf` class provides common functionality for "
"recipes that need to install GConf schemas. The schemas will be put into "
"a separate package (``${``\\ :term:`PN`\\ ``}-gconf``) that is created "
"automatically when this class is inherited. This package uses the "
"appropriate post-install and post-remove (postinst/postrm) scriptlets to "
"register and unregister the schemas in the target image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:893
msgid "``gettext``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:895
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-gettext` class provides support for building "
"software that uses the GNU ``gettext`` internationalization and "
"localization system. All recipes building software that use ``gettext`` "
"should inherit this class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:900
msgid "This class will configure recipes to build translations *unless*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:902
msgid "the :term:`USE_NLS` variable is set to ``no``, or"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:904
msgid ""
"the :term:`INHIBIT_DEFAULT_DEPS` variable is set and the recipe "
"inheriting the :ref:`ref-classes-gettext` class does not also inherit the"
" :ref:`ref-classes-cross-canadian` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:911
msgid "``github-releases``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:913
msgid ""
"For recipes that fetch release tarballs from github, the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-github-releases` class sets up a standard way for checking "
"available upstream versions (to support ``devtool upgrade`` and the "
"Automated Upgrade Helper (AUH))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:917
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"To use it, add \":ref:`ref-classes-github-releases`\" to the inherit line"
" in the recipe, and if the default value of :term:`GITHUB_BASE_URI` is "
"not suitable, then set your own value in the recipe. You should then use "
"``${GITHUB_BASE_URI}`` in the value you set for :term:`SRC_URI` within "
"the recipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:925
msgid "``gnomebase``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:927
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-gnomebase` class is the base class for recipes that"
" build software from the GNOME stack. This class sets :term:`SRC_URI` to "
"download the source from the GNOME mirrors as well as extending "
":term:`FILES` with the typical GNOME installation paths."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:936
msgid "``go``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:938
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-go` class supports building Go programs. The "
"behavior of this class is controlled by the mandatory :term:`GO_IMPORT` "
"variable, and by the optional :term:`GO_INSTALL` and "
":term:`GO_INSTALL_FILTEROUT` ones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:942
msgid ""
"To build a Go program with the Yocto Project, you can use the :oe_git"
":`go-helloworld_0.1.bb </openembedded-core/tree/meta/recipes-extended/go-"
"examples/go-helloworld_0.1.bb>` recipe as an example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:949
msgid "``go-mod``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:951
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-go-mod` class allows to use Go modules, and "
"inherits the :ref:`ref-classes-go` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:954
msgid "See the associated :term:`GO_WORKDIR` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:959
msgid "``go-mod-update-modules``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:961
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-go-mod-update-modules` class can be used in Go "
"recipes and defines a ``do_update_modules`` task that can be run manually"
" to update two files (\"BPN\" below corresponds to :term:`BPN`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:965
msgid "``BPN-go-mods.inc``: list of Go modules the recipe depends on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:966
msgid ""
"``BPN-licenses.inc``: list of licenses for each Go modules the recipe "
"depends on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:969
msgid ""
"These files can then updated automatically with the ``do_update_modules``"
" task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:974
msgid "``go-vendor``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:976
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-go-vendor` class implements support for offline "
"builds, also known as Go vendoring. In such a scenario, the module "
"dependencias are downloaded during the :ref:`ref-tasks-fetch` task rather"
" than when modules are imported, thus being coherent with Yocto's concept"
" of fetching every source beforehand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:982
msgid ""
"The dependencies are unpacked into the modules' ``vendor`` directory, "
"where a manifest file is generated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:988
msgid "``gobject-introspection``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:990
msgid ""
"Provides support for recipes building software that supports GObject "
"introspection. This functionality is only enabled if the \"gobject-"
"introspection-data\" feature is in :term:`DISTRO_FEATURES` as well as "
"\"qemu-usermode\" being in :term:`MACHINE_FEATURES`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:999
msgid ""
"This functionality is :ref:`enabled <ref-manual/features:Default "
"Features>` by default and, if not applicable, should be disabled through "
":term:`DISTRO_FEATURES_OPTED_OUT` or :term:`MACHINE_FEATURES_OPTED_OUT`, "
"respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1007
msgid "``grub-efi``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1009
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-grub-efi` class provides ``grub-efi``-specific "
"functions for building bootable images."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1012
msgid "This class supports several variables:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1014
msgid ""
":term:`INITRD`: Indicates list of filesystem images to concatenate and "
"use as an initial RAM disk (initrd) (optional). Can be specified for each"
" ``LABEL``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1018
msgid ""
":term:`ROOTFS`: Indicates a filesystem image to include as the root "
"filesystem (optional)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1021
msgid ""
":term:`GRUB_GFXSERIAL`: Set this to \"1\" to have graphics and serial in "
"the boot menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1024
msgid ":term:`LABELS`: A list of targets for the automatic configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1027
msgid ":term:`APPEND`: An override list of append strings for each ``LABEL``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1030
msgid ""
":term:`GRUB_TITLE`: A custom title for each ``LABEL``. If a label does "
"not have a custom title, the label is used as title for the GRUB menu "
"entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1033
msgid ""
":term:`GRUB_OPTS`: Additional options to add to the configuration "
"(optional). Options are delimited using semi-colon characters (``;``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1037
msgid ""
":term:`GRUB_TIMEOUT`: Timeout before executing the default ``LABEL`` "
"(optional)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1040
msgid ""
"Each ``LABEL`` defined in the :term:`LABELS` variable creates a GRUB boot"
" entry, and some variables can be defined individually per ``LABEL``. The"
" label specific override names are defined as ``grub_LABEL``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1044
msgid ""
"For example, for a label ``factory``, the override name would be "
"``grub_factory``. A custom GRUB menu entry titled \"Factory Install\" "
"with the additional parameter ``factory=yes`` can be achieved as "
"follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1055
msgid "``gsettings``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1057
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-gsettings` class provides common functionality for "
"recipes that need to install GSettings (glib) schemas. The schemas are "
"assumed to be part of the main package. Appropriate post-install and "
"post-remove (postinst/postrm) scriptlets are added to register and "
"unregister the schemas in the target image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1066
msgid "``gtk-doc``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1068
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-gtk-doc` class is a helper class to pull in the "
"appropriate ``gtk-doc`` dependencies and disable ``gtk-doc``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1074
msgid "``gtk-icon-cache``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1076
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-gtk-icon-cache` class generates the proper post-"
"install and post-remove (postinst/postrm) scriptlets for packages that "
"use GTK+ and install icons. These scriptlets call ``gtk-update-icon-"
"cache`` to add the fonts to GTK+'s icon cache. Since the cache files are "
"architecture-specific, ``gtk-update-icon-cache`` is run using QEMU if the"
" postinst scriptlets need to be run on the build host during image "
"creation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1087
msgid "``gtk-immodules-cache``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1089
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-gtk-immodules-cache` class generates the proper "
"post-install and post-remove (postinst/postrm) scriptlets for packages "
"that install GTK+ input method modules for virtual keyboards. These "
"scriptlets call ``gtk-update-icon-cache`` to add the input method modules"
" to the cache. Since the cache files are architecture-specific, ``gtk-"
"update-icon-cache`` is run using QEMU if the postinst scriptlets need to "
"be run on the build host during image creation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1097
msgid ""
"If the input method modules being installed are in packages other than "
"the main package, set :term:`GTKIMMODULES_PACKAGES` to specify the "
"packages containing the modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1105
msgid "``gzipnative``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1107
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-gzipnative` class enables the use of different "
"native versions of ``gzip`` and ``pigz`` rather than the versions of "
"these tools from the build host."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1114
msgid "``image``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1116
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-image` class helps support creating images in "
"different formats. First, the root filesystem is created from packages "
"using one of the ``rootfs*.bbclass`` files (depending on the package "
"format used) and then one or more image files are created."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1121
msgid ""
"The :term:`IMAGE_FSTYPES` variable controls the types of images to "
"generate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1124
msgid ""
"The :term:`IMAGE_INSTALL` variable controls the list of packages to "
"install into the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1127
msgid ""
"For information on customizing images, see the \":ref:`dev-manual"
"/customizing-images:customizing images`\" section in the Yocto Project "
"Development Tasks Manual. For information on how images are created, see "
"the \":ref:`overview-manual/concepts:images`\" section in the Yocto "
"Project Overview and Concepts Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1137
msgid "``image-buildinfo``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1139
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-image-buildinfo` class writes a plain text file "
"containing build information to the target filesystem at "
"``${sysconfdir}/buildinfo`` by default (as specified by "
":term:`IMAGE_BUILDINFO_FILE`). This can be useful for manually "
"determining the origin of any given image. It writes out two sections:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1145
msgid ""
"`Build Configuration`: a list of variables and their values (specified by"
" :term:`IMAGE_BUILDINFO_VARS`, which defaults to :term:`DISTRO` and "
":term:`DISTRO_VERSION`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1149
msgid "`Layer Revisions`: the revisions of all of the layers used in the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1152
msgid ""
"Additionally, when building an SDK it will write the same contents to "
"``/buildinfo`` by default (as specified by :term:`SDK_BUILDINFO_FILE`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1159
msgid "``image_types``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1161
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-image_types` class defines all of the standard "
"image output types that you can enable through the :term:`IMAGE_FSTYPES` "
"variable. You can use this class as a reference on how to add support for"
" custom image output types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1167
msgid ""
"By default, the :ref:`ref-classes-image` class automatically enables the "
":ref:`ref-classes-image_types` class. The :ref:`ref-classes-image` class "
"uses the ``IMGCLASSES`` variable as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1184
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-image_types` class also handles conversion and "
"compression of images."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1188
msgid ""
"To build a VMware VMDK image, you need to add \"wic.vmdk\" to "
":term:`IMAGE_FSTYPES`. This would also be similar for Virtual Box Virtual"
" Disk Image (\"vdi\") and QEMU Copy On Write Version 2 (\"qcow2\") "
"images."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1195
msgid "``image-container``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1197
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-image-container` class is automatically inherited "
"in :doc:`image </ref-manual/images>` recipes that have the ``container`` "
"image type in :term:`IMAGE_FSTYPES`. It provides relevant settings to "
"generate an image ready for use with an :wikipedia:`OCI "
"<Open_Container_Initiative>`-compliant container management tool, such as"
" :wikipedia:`Podman <Podman>` or :wikipedia:`Docker <Docker_(software)>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1206
msgid ""
"This class neither builds nor installs container management tools on the "
"target. Those tools are available in the :yocto_git:`meta-virtualization "
"</meta-virtualization>` layer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1210
msgid ""
"You should set the :term:`PREFERRED_PROVIDER` for the Linux kernel to "
"``linux-dummy`` in a :term:`configuration file`::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1215
msgid ""
"Otherwise an error is triggered. If desired, the "
":term:`IMAGE_CONTAINER_NO_DUMMY` variable can be set to \"1\" to avoid "
"triggering this error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1219
msgid ""
"The ``linux-dummy`` recipe acts as a Linux kernel recipe but builds "
"nothing. It is relevant to use as the preferred Linux kernel provider in "
"this case as a container image does not need to include a Linux kernel. "
"Selecting it as the preferred provider for the kernel will also decrease "
"build time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1224
msgid ""
"Using this class only deploys an additional ``tar.bz2`` archive to "
":term:`DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE`. This archive can be used in a container file (a"
" file typically named ``Dockerfile`` or ``Containerfile``). For example, "
"to be used with :wikipedia:`Podman <Podman>` or :wikipedia:`Docker "
"<Docker_(software)>`, the `container file "
"<https://docs.docker.com/reference/dockerfile/>`__ could contain the "
"following instructions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1237
msgid ""
"This is suitable to build a container using our generated root filesystem"
" image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1242
msgid "``image-live``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1244
msgid ""
"This class controls building \"live\" (i.e. HDDIMG and ISO) images. Live "
"images contain syslinux for legacy booting, as well as the bootloader "
"specified by :term:`EFI_PROVIDER` if :term:`MACHINE_FEATURES` contains "
"\"efi\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1249
msgid ""
"Normally, you do not use this class directly. Instead, you add \"live\" "
"to :term:`IMAGE_FSTYPES`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1255
msgid "``insane``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1257
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-insane` class adds a step to the package generation"
" process so that output quality assurance checks are generated by the "
"OpenEmbedded build system. A range of checks are performed that check the"
" build's output for common problems that show up during runtime. "
"Distribution policy usually dictates whether to include this class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1263
msgid ""
"You can configure the sanity checks so that specific test failures either"
" raise a warning or an error message. Typically, failures for new tests "
"generate a warning. Subsequent failures for the same test would then "
"generate an error message once the metadata is in a known and good "
"condition. See the \":doc:`/ref-manual/qa-checks`\" Chapter for a list of"
" all the warning and error messages you might encounter using a default "
"configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1270
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Use the :term:`WARN_QA` and :term:`ERROR_QA` variables to control the "
"behavior of these checks at the global level (i.e. in your custom distro "
"configuration). However, to skip one or more checks in recipes, you "
"should use :term:`INSANE_SKIP`. For example, to skip the check for "
"symbolic link ``.so`` files in the main package of a recipe, add the "
"following to the recipe. You need to realize that the package name "
"override, in this example ``${PN}``, must be used::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1281 ../../ref-manual/qa-checks.rst:1045
msgid ""
"Please keep in mind that the QA checks are meant to detect real or "
"potential problems in the packaged output. So exercise caution when "
"disabling these checks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1285
msgid ""
"The tests you can list with the :term:`WARN_QA` and :term:`ERROR_QA` "
"variables are documented in the :doc:`/ref-manual/qa-checks` document of "
"the Yocto Project Reference Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1292
msgid "``kernel``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1294
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel` class handles building Linux kernels. The "
"class contains code to build all kernel trees. All needed headers are "
"staged into the :term:`STAGING_KERNEL_DIR` directory to allow out-of-tree"
" module builds using the :ref:`ref-classes-module` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1299
msgid ""
"If a file named ``defconfig`` is listed in :term:`SRC_URI`, then by "
"default :ref:`ref-tasks-configure` copies it as ``.config`` in the build "
"directory, so it is automatically used as the kernel configuration for "
"the build. This copy is not performed in case ``.config`` already exists "
"there: this allows recipes to produce a configuration by other means in "
"``do_configure:prepend``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1306
msgid ""
"Each built kernel module is packaged separately and inter-module "
"dependencies are created by parsing the ``modinfo`` output.  If all "
"modules are required, then installing the ``kernel-modules`` package "
"installs all packages with modules and various other kernel packages such"
" as ``kernel-vmlinux``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1312
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel` class contains logic that allows you to "
"embed an initial RAM filesystem (:term:`Initramfs`) image when you build "
"the kernel image. For information on how to build an :term:`Initramfs`, "
"see the \":ref:`dev-manual/building:building an initial ram filesystem "
"(Initramfs) image`\" section in the Yocto Project Development Tasks "
"Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1318
msgid ""
"Various other classes are used by the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel` and :ref"
":`ref-classes-module` classes internally including the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"kernel-arch` and :ref:`ref-classes-module-base` classes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1324
msgid "``kernel-arch``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1326
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-arch` class sets the ``ARCH`` environment "
"variable for Linux kernel compilation (including modules)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1332
msgid "``kernel-devicetree``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1334
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-devicetree` class, which is inherited by the"
" :ref:`ref-classes-kernel` class, supports device tree generation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1337
msgid "Its behavior is mainly controlled by the following variables:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1339
msgid ""
":term:`KERNEL_DEVICETREE_BUNDLE`: whether to bundle the kernel and device"
" tree"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1340
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_DTBDEST`: directory where to install DTB files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1341
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_DTBVENDORED`: whether to keep vendor subdirectories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1342
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_DTC_FLAGS`: flags for ``dtc``, the Device Tree Compiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1343
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_PACKAGE_NAME`: base name of the kernel packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1348
msgid "``kernel-fit-image``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1350
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image` class provides support to pack a "
"kernel image, device trees, a U-boot script, an :term:`Initramfs` and "
"additional loadables into a single FIT image. In theory, a FIT image can "
"support any number of kernels, U-boot scripts, :term:`Initramfs`, and "
"device trees. However, :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image` currently only"
" supports limited usecases: just one kernel image, an optional U-boot "
"script, an optional :term:`Initramfs`, and any number of device trees and"
" loadables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1359
msgid ""
"The FIT image is created by a recipe which inherits the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-kernel-fit-image` class. One such example is the ``linux-yocto-"
"fitimage`` recipe which creates a FIT image for the Linux Yocto kernel. "
"Additionally, it is required that :term:`KERNEL_CLASSES` is set to "
"include :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-extra-artifacts`. The :ref:`ref-"
"classes-kernel-fit-extra-artifacts` class exposes the required kernel "
"artifacts to the :term:`DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE` which are used by the :ref"
":`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image` class to create the FIT image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1369
msgid "The simplest example for building a FIT image is to add::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1373
msgid "to the machine :term:`configuration file` and to execute::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1377
msgid ""
"This results in a ``fitImage`` file deployed to the "
":term:`DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE` directory and a ``linux-yocto-fitimage`` package"
" which can be installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1380
msgid ""
"The same approach works for all variants of the ``linux-yocto`` kernel. "
"For example, if the ``linux-yocto-rt`` kernel should be used, add the "
"following lines to the machine configuration file::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1387
msgid ""
"The FIT image, this time including the RT kernel, is built again by "
"calling::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1391
msgid ""
"For other kernels provided by other layers, the same approach would work."
" However, it is usually more intuitive to add a custom FIT image recipe "
"next to the custom kernel recipe. For example, if a layer provides a "
"``linux-vanilla`` recipe, a ``linux-vanilla-fitimage`` recipe may be "
"added as well. The ``linux-vanilla-fitimage`` recipe can be created as a "
"customized copy of the ``linux-yocto-fitimage`` recipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1399
msgid ""
"Usually the kernel is built as a dependency of an image. If the FIT image"
" should be used as a replacement for the kernel image which is installed "
"in the root filesystem, then the following variables can be set e.g. in "
"the machine configuration file::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1416
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image` class processes several variables"
" that allow configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1419
msgid ""
"The options for the device tree compiler passed to ``mkimage -D`` when "
"creating the FIT image are specified using the "
":term:`UBOOT_MKIMAGE_DTCOPTS` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1423
msgid ""
"Only a single kernel can be added to the FIT image created by :ref:`ref-"
"classes-kernel-fit-image` and it is a mandatory component of the FIT "
"image. The address where the kernel image is to be loaded by U-Boot is "
"specified by :term:`UBOOT_LOADADDRESS` and the entrypoint by "
":term:`UBOOT_ENTRYPOINT`. Setting :term:`FIT_ADDRESS_CELLS` to \"2\" is "
"necessary if such addresses are 64 bit ones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1431
msgid ""
"Multiple device trees can be added to the FIT image created by :ref:`ref-"
"classes-kernel-fit-image` and the device tree is optional. The address "
"where the device tree is to be loaded by U-Boot is specified by "
":term:`UBOOT_DTBO_LOADADDRESS` for device tree overlays and by "
":term:`UBOOT_DTB_LOADADDRESS` for device tree binaries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1437
msgid ""
"Only a single :term:`Initramfs` can be added to the FIT image created by "
":ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image`. The :term:`Initramfs` in FIT is "
"optional. The address where the RAM disk image is to be loaded by U-Boot "
"is specified by :term:`UBOOT_RD_LOADADDRESS` and the entrypoint by "
":term:`UBOOT_RD_ENTRYPOINT`. The :term:`Initramfs` is added to the FIT "
"image when :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` is specified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1444
msgid ""
"It's recommended to add the :term:`Initramfs` and the kernel image as "
"independent image nodes to the FIT image. Bundling a RAM disk image with "
"the kernel image and including the bundle (:term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE_BUNDLE`"
" set to \"1\") in the FIT image is possible. However, this approach has "
"the disadvantage that any change to the RAM disk image necessitates "
"rebuilding the kernel image. This process requires the full kernel build "
"directory, which is kind of incompatible with the :term:`SSTATE_DIR` and,"
" consequently, with SDKs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1453
msgid ""
"Only a single U-Boot boot script can be added to the FIT image created by"
" :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image`. The boot script is optional. The "
"boot script is specified in the ITS file as a text file containing U-Boot"
" commands. When using a boot script the recipe which inherits the :ref"
":`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image` class should add the script to "
":term:`SRC_URI` and set the :term:`FIT_UBOOT_ENV` variable to the name of"
" the file like the following::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1464
msgid ""
"At run-time, U-boot's boot command can be configured to load the boot "
"script from the FIT image and source it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1467
msgid ""
"Multiple additional loadables (e.g.: firmwares for auxiliary processors) "
"can be added to the FIT image created by :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-"
"image`. The addition of loadables is optional. The loadables are "
"specified using the :term:`FIT_LOADABLES` variable; each of them can then"
" be configured through flags on the following variables: "
":term:`FIT_LOADABLE_ARCH`, :term:`FIT_LOADABLE_COMPRESSION`, "
":term:`FIT_LOADABLE_DESCRIPTION`, :term:`FIT_LOADABLE_ENTRYPOINT`, "
":term:`FIT_LOADABLE_FILENAME`, :term:`FIT_LOADABLE_LOADADDRESS`, "
":term:`FIT_LOADABLE_OS` and :term:`FIT_LOADABLE_TYPE`. All loadables "
"specified in this way will be added to all configurations present in the "
"FIT image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1478
msgid ""
"The FIT image generated by the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image` class "
"is signed when the variables :term:`UBOOT_SIGN_ENABLE`, "
":term:`UBOOT_MKIMAGE_DTCOPTS`, :term:`UBOOT_SIGN_KEYDIR` and "
":term:`UBOOT_SIGN_KEYNAME` are set appropriately. The default values used"
" for :term:`FIT_HASH_ALG` and :term:`FIT_SIGN_ALG` in :ref:`ref-classes-"
"kernel-fit-image` are \"sha256\" and \"rsa2048\" respectively. The keys "
"for signing the FIT image can be generated using the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"kernel-fit-image` class when both :term:`FIT_GENERATE_KEYS` and "
":term:`UBOOT_SIGN_ENABLE` are set to \"1\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1490
msgid "``kernel-fit-extra-artifacts``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1492
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-extra-artifacts` class exposes the "
"required kernel artifacts to the :term:`DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE` directory. "
"These artifacts are used by the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image` class"
" to create a FIT image that can include the kernel, device trees, an "
"optional U-Boot script, and an optional Initramfs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1498
msgid ""
"This class is typically included by adding it to the "
":term:`KERNEL_CLASSES` variable in your kernel recipe or machine "
"configuration when building FIT images. It ensures that all necessary "
"files are available for packaging into the FIT image, such as the kernel "
"binary, device tree blobs (DTBs), and other related files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1503
msgid "For example, to enable this class, set::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1507
msgid ""
"This is required when using the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-image` class"
" to generate FIT images for your kernel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1513
msgid "``kernel-grub``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1515
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-grub` class updates the boot area and the "
"boot menu with the kernel as the priority boot mechanism while installing"
" a RPM to update the kernel on a deployed target."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1522
msgid "``kernel-module-split``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1524
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-module-split` class provides common "
"functionality for splitting Linux kernel modules into separate packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1530
msgid "``kernel-uboot``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1532
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-uboot` class provides support for building "
"from vmlinux-style kernel sources."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1538
msgid "``kernel-uimage``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1540
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-uimage` class provides support to pack "
"uImage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1545
msgid "``kernel-yocto``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1547
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-yocto` class provides common functionality "
"for building from linux-yocto style kernel source repositories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1553
msgid "``kernel-yocto-rust``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1555
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-yocto-rust` class creates the necessary Rust"
" Linux kernel dependencies and ensures that they are available by running"
" ``make rustavailable`` from the Linux kernel source tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1562
msgid "``kernelsrc``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1564
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-kernelsrc` class sets the Linux kernel source and "
"version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1569
msgid "``lib_package``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1571
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-lib_package` class supports recipes that build "
"libraries and produce executable binaries, where those binaries should "
"not be installed by default along with the library. Instead, the binaries"
" are added to a separate ``${``\\ :term:`PN`\\ ``}-bin`` package to make "
"their installation optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1580
msgid "``libc*``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1582
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-libc*` classes support recipes that build packages "
"with ``libc``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1584
msgid ""
"The :ref:`libc-common <ref-classes-libc*>` class provides common support "
"for building with ``libc``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1587
msgid ""
"The :ref:`libc-package <ref-classes-libc*>` class supports packaging up "
"``glibc`` and ``eglibc``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1593
msgid "``license``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1595
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-license` class provides license manifest creation "
"and license exclusion. This class is enabled by default using the default"
" value for the :term:`INHERIT_DISTRO` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1602
msgid "``linuxloader``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1604
msgid ""
"Provides the function ``linuxloader()``, which gives the value of the "
"dynamic loader/linker provided on the platform. This value is used by a "
"number of other classes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1611
msgid "``logging``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1613
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-logging` class provides the standard shell "
"functions used to log messages for various BitBake severity levels (i.e. "
"``bbplain``, ``bbnote``, ``bbwarn``, ``bberror``, ``bbfatal``, and "
"``bbdebug``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1617 ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1685
msgid ""
"This class is enabled by default since it is inherited by the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-base` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1623
msgid "``meson``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1625
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-meson` class allows to create recipes that build "
"software using the `Meson <https://mesonbuild.com/>`__ build system. You "
"can use the :term:`MESON_BUILDTYPE`, :term:`MESON_TARGET` and "
":term:`EXTRA_OEMESON` variables to specify additional configuration "
"options to be passed using the ``meson`` command line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1634
msgid "``metadata_scm``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1636
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-metadata_scm` class provides functionality for "
"querying the branch and revision of a Source Code Manager (SCM) "
"repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1639
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-base` class uses this class to print the revisions "
"of each layer before starting every build. The :ref:`ref-classes-"
"metadata_scm` class is enabled by default because it is inherited by the "
":ref:`ref-classes-base` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1647 ../../ref-manual/qa-checks.rst:474
msgid "``mime``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1649
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-mime` class generates the proper post-install and "
"post-remove (postinst/postrm) scriptlets for packages that install MIME "
"type files. These scriptlets call ``update-mime-database`` to add the "
"MIME types to the shared database."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1657 ../../ref-manual/qa-checks.rst:487
msgid "``mime-xdg``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1659
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-mime-xdg` class generates the proper post-install "
"and post-remove (postinst/postrm) scriptlets for packages that install "
"``.desktop`` files containing ``MimeType`` entries. These scriptlets call"
" ``update-desktop-database`` to add the MIME types to the database of "
"MIME types handled by desktop files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1665
msgid ""
"Thanks to this class, when users open a file through a file browser on "
"recently created images, they don't have to choose the application to "
"open the file from the pool of all known applications, even the ones that"
" cannot open the selected file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1670
msgid ""
"If you have recipes installing their ``.desktop`` files as absolute "
"symbolic links, the detection of such files cannot be done by the current"
" implementation of this class. In this case, you have to add the "
"corresponding package names to the :term:`MIME_XDG_PACKAGES` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1678
msgid "``mirrors``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1680
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-mirrors` class sets up some standard "
":term:`MIRRORS` entries for source code mirrors. These mirrors provide a "
"fall-back path in case the upstream source specified in :term:`SRC_URI` "
"within recipes is unavailable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1691
msgid "``module``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1693
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-module` class provides support for building out-of-"
"tree Linux kernel modules. The class inherits the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"module-base` and :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-module-split` classes, and "
"implements the :ref:`ref-tasks-compile` and :ref:`ref-tasks-install` "
"tasks. The class provides everything needed to build and package a kernel"
" module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1699 ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1723
msgid ""
"For general information on out-of-tree Linux kernel modules, see the "
"\":ref:`kernel-dev/common:incorporating out-of-tree modules`\" section in"
" the Yocto Project Linux Kernel Development Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1706
msgid "``module-base``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1708
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-module-base` class provides the base functionality "
"for building Linux kernel modules. Typically, a recipe that builds "
"software that includes one or more kernel modules and has its own means "
"of building the module inherits this class as opposed to inheriting the "
":ref:`ref-classes-module` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1717
msgid "``module-rust``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1719
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-module-rust` class provides support for building "
"out-of-tree Linux kernel modules written in the Rust programming "
"language. The class inherits the :ref:`ref-classes-module` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1730
msgid "``multilib*``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1732
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-classes-multilib*` classes provide support for building "
"libraries with different target optimizations or target architectures and"
" installing them side-by-side in the same image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/classes.rst:1736
msgid ""
"For more information on using the Multilib feature, see the \":ref:`dev-"
"manual/libraries:combining multiple versions of library files into one "
"image`\" section in the Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

