"The target is the address of the target machine, which must be running an"
" SSH server (i.e. ``user@hostname[:destdir]``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:525
msgid ""
"This command deploys all files installed during the :ref:`ref-tasks-"
"install` task. Furthermore, you do not need to have package management "
"enabled within the target machine. If you do, the package manager is "
"bypassed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:532
msgid ""
"The ``deploy-target`` functionality is for development only. You should "
"never use it to update an image that will be used in production."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:536
msgid ""
"Some conditions could prevent a deployed application from behaving as "
"expected. When both of the following conditions are met, your application"
" has the potential to not behave correctly when run on the target:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:541
msgid ""
"You are deploying a new application to the target and the recipe you used"
" to build the application had correctly defined runtime dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:545
msgid ""
"The target does not physically have the packages on which the application"
" depends installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:548
msgid ""
"If both of these conditions are met, your application will not behave as "
"expected. The reason for this misbehavior is because the ``devtool "
"deploy-target`` command does not deploy the packages (e.g. libraries) on "
"which your new application depends. The assumption is that the packages "
"are already on the target. Consequently, when a runtime call is made in "
"the application for a dependent function (e.g. a library call), the "
"function cannot be found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:558
msgid ""
"Runtime dependencies can be explicitly listed in the :term:`RDEPENDS` "
"variable, but may also be the result of a :term:`DEPENDS` assignment in "
"your application's recipe. This is usually the case when your application"
" depends on libraries for compilation: these libraries are listed as "
"build-time dependencies in the :term:`DEPENDS` variable in your "
"application's recipe. However these may also be runtime dependencies if "
"they install shared objects on which your application will dynamically "
"link to at runtime (e.g. shared libraries ending with ``.so``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:567
msgid ""
"These runtime dependencies are automatically resolved by the "
":term:`OpenEmbedded Build System` during the packaging phase. Since "
"``devtool`` ignores packaging dependencies, they will not be installed "
"automatically with ``devtool deploy-target``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:572
msgid ""
"For more information on how the :term:`OpenEmbedded Build System` handles"
" packaging, see the :ref:`overview-manual/concepts:Automatically Added "
"Runtime Dependencies` section of the Yocto Project Overview and Concepts "
"Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:576
msgid ""
"To be sure you have all the dependencies local to the target, you need to"
" be sure that the packages are pre-deployed (installed) on the target "
"before attempting to run your application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:583
msgid "Removing Your Software from the Target Machine"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:585
msgid ""
"Use the ``devtool undeploy-target`` command to remove deployed build "
"output from the target machine. For the ``devtool undeploy-target`` "
"command to work, you must have previously used the \":ref:`devtool "
"deploy-target <ref-manual/devtool-reference:deploying your software on "
"the target machine>`\" command::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/devtool-reference.rst:593
msgid ""
"The target is the address of the target machine, which must be running an"
" SSH server (i.e. ``user@hostname``)."
msgstr ""

