#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:13
msgid "How does Poky differ from OpenEmbedded?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:15
msgid ""
"The term :term:`Poky` refers to the reference distribution that the Yocto"
" Project provides, and a reference test configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:18
msgid ""
"The :term:`Poky` distribution is built using the :term:`OpenEmbedded "
"Build System` and :term:`BitBake`. The :term:`metadata` involved to build"
" the :term:`Poky` reference distribution is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:22
msgid ""
":term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)`, which is hosted at :oe_git"
":`/openembedded-core`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:25
msgid ""
":yocto_git:`meta-poky </meta-yocto/tree/meta-poky>`, which defines the "
"``poky`` distro configuration file (:term:`DISTRO`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:30 ../../ref-manual/terms.rst:482
msgid ""
"Poky also used to be a repository containing the combination of "
":term:`BitBake`, :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)`, the :yocto_git"
":`meta-yocto </meta-yocto>` repository and the Yocto Project "
"documentation -- which were combined with the :yocto_git:`combo-layer "
"</poky-config>` tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:36 ../../ref-manual/terms.rst:488
msgid ""
"This repository is still hosted at :yocto_git:`/poky` and is still "
"updated for maintained releases that are older than Whinlatter (5.3). "
"Newer releases now use ``bitbake-setup`` to clone and setup the initial "
":term:`OpenEmbedded Build System` environment. For more information on "
"how to use ``bitbake-setup``, see the :doc:`/brief-yoctoprojectqs/index` "
"document. An alternative can be to setup the above repositories manually,"
" by following the :doc:`/dev-manual/poky-manual-setup` section of the "
"Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:46
msgid "How can you claim Poky / OpenEmbedded-Core is stable?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:48
msgid "There are three areas that help with stability;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:50
msgid ""
"The Yocto Project team keeps :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)` small "
"and focused, containing around a thousand recipes as opposed to the "
"thousands available in other OpenEmbedded community layers. Keeping it "
"small makes it easy to test and maintain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:55
msgid ""
"The Yocto Project team runs manual and automated tests using a small, "
"fixed set of reference hardware as well as emulated targets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:58
msgid ""
"The Yocto Project uses an :yocto_ab:`autobuilder <>`, which provides "
"continuous build and integration tests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:62
msgid "Are there any products built using the OpenEmbedded build system?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:64
msgid ""
"See :yocto_wiki:`Products that use the Yocto Project "
"</Project_Users#Products_that_use_the_Yocto_Project>` in the Yocto "
"Project Wiki. Don't hesitate to contribute to this page if you know other"
" such products."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:70
msgid "Why isn't systemd the default init system in the Poky distro?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:72
msgid ""
"`systemd <https://systemd.io/>`__ is a desktop Linux init system with a "
"specific focus that is not entirely aligned with a customisable "
"\"embedded\" build system/environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:76
msgid ""
"It understandably mandates certain layouts and configurations which may "
"or may not align with what the objectives and direction :term"
":`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)` or Yocto Project want to take. It doesn't "
"support all of our targets. For example `musl <https://www.musl-"
"libc.org/>`__ support in systemd is problematic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:82
msgid ""
"If it were our default, we would have to align with all their choices and"
" this doesn't make sense. It is therefore a configuration option and "
"available to anyone where the design goals align. But we are clear it is "
"not the only way to handle init."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:87
msgid ""
"Our automated testing includes it through the ``poky-altcfg`` "
":term:`DISTRO` and we don't really need it to be the default: it is "
"tested, it works, and people can choose to use it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:92
msgid "Building environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:95
msgid "Missing dependencies on the development system?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:97
msgid ""
"If your development system does not meet the required Git, tar, and "
"Python versions, you can get the required tools on your host development "
"system in different ways (i.e. building a tarball or downloading a "
"tarball). See the \":ref:`ref-manual/system-requirements:required git, "
"tar, python, make and gcc versions`\" section for steps on how to update "
"your build tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:104
msgid ""
"How does OpenEmbedded fetch source code? Will it work through a firewall "
"or proxy server?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:106
msgid ""
"The way the build system obtains source code is highly configurable. You "
"can setup the build system to get source code in most environments if "
"HTTP transport is available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:110
msgid ""
"When the build system searches for source code, it first tries the local "
"download directory (:term:`DL_DIR`). If that location fails, the "
":term:`OpenEmbedded Build System` tries :term:`PREMIRRORS`, the upstream "
"source (specified in :term:`SRC_URI`), and then :term:`MIRRORS` in that "
"order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:115
msgid ""
"Assuming your distribution is \"poky\", the OpenEmbedded build system "
"uses the Yocto Project source :term:`PREMIRRORS` by default for SCM-based"
" sources, upstreams for normal tarballs, and then falls back to a number "
"of other mirrors including the Yocto Project source mirror if those fail."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:121
msgid ""
"As an example, you could add a specific server for the build system to "
"attempt before any others by adding something like the following to a "
":term:`configuration file`::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/faq.rst:132 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7931
