#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:5
msgid "Source Directory Structure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:7
msgid ""
"The :term:`Source Directory` consists of numerous files, directories and "
"subdirectories; understanding their locations and contents is key to "
"using the Yocto Project effectively. This chapter describes the Source "
"Directory and gives information about those files and directories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:15 ../../ref-manual/terms.rst:571
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system does not support file or directory names "
"that contain spaces. Be sure that the Source Directory you use does not "
"contain these types of names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:19
msgid ""
"For the following sections, we assume the core components of the "
":term:`Source Directory` are organized as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:35
msgid "Top-Level Core Components"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:37
msgid ""
"This section describes the top-level components of the :term:`Source "
"Directory`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:42
msgid "``bitbake/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:44
msgid ""
"This directory includes a copy of BitBake for ease of use. The copy "
"usually matches the current stable BitBake release from the BitBake "
"project. BitBake, a :term:`Metadata` interpreter, reads the Yocto Project"
" Metadata and runs the tasks defined by that data. Failures are usually "
"caused by errors in your Metadata and not from BitBake itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:51
msgid ""
"When you run the ``bitbake`` command, the main BitBake executable (which "
"resides in the ``bitbake/bin/`` directory) starts. Sourcing the "
"environment setup script (i.e. :ref:`structure-core-script`) places the "
"``scripts/`` and ``bitbake/bin/`` directories (in that order) into the "
"shell's ``PATH`` environment variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:57
msgid ""
"For more information on BitBake, see the :doc:`BitBake User Manual "
"<bitbake:index>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:63
msgid "``build/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:65
msgid ""
"This directory contains user configuration files and the output generated"
" by the OpenEmbedded build system in its standard configuration where the"
" source tree is combined with the output. The :term:`Build Directory` is "
"created initially when you ``source`` the OpenEmbedded build environment "
"setup script (i.e. :ref:`structure-core-script`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:71
msgid ""
"It is also possible to place output and configuration files in a "
"directory separate from the :term:`Source Directory` by providing a "
"directory name when you ``source`` the setup script. For information on "
"separating output from your local Source Directory files (commonly "
"described as an \"out of tree\" build), see the \":ref:`structure-core-"
"script`\" section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:78
msgid ""
"See the \":ref:`The Build Directory --- build/ <structure-build>`\" "
"section for details about the contents of the :term:`Build Directory`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:84
msgid "``layers/yocto-docs/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:86
msgid "The Yocto documentation repository is hosted at :yocto_git:`/yocto-docs`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:88
msgid ""
"This directory holds the source for the Yocto Project documentation as "
"well as templates and tools that allow you to generate PDF and HTML "
"versions of the manuals. Each manual is contained in its own sub-folder; "
"for example, the files for this reference manual reside in the ``ref-"
"manual/`` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:94
msgid "This directory is not mandatory for building with :term:`BitBake`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:97
msgid "``layers/openembedded-core/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:99
msgid ""
"The :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)` repository is hosted at :oe_git"
":`/openembedded-core`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:105
msgid "``meta/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:107
msgid ""
"This directory contains the minimal, underlying :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core "
"(OE-Core)` metadata. The directory holds recipes, common classes, and "
"machine configuration for strictly emulated targets (``qemux86``, "
"``qemuarm``, and so forth.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:115
msgid "``meta-selftest/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:117
msgid ""
"This directory adds additional recipes and append files used by the "
"OpenEmbedded selftests to verify the behavior of the build system. You do"
" not have to add this layer to your :ref:`structure-build-conf-"
"bblayers.conf` file unless you want to run the selftests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:125
msgid "``meta-skeleton/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:127
msgid "This directory contains template recipes for BSP and kernel development."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:132
msgid "``scripts/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:134
msgid ""
"This directory contains various integration scripts that implement extra "
"functionality in the Yocto Project environment (e.g. QEMU scripts). The "
":ref:`structure-core-script` script prepends this directory to the "
"shell's ``PATH`` environment variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:139
msgid ""
"The ``scripts`` directory has useful scripts that assist in contributing "
"back to the Yocto Project, such as ``create-pull-request`` and ``send-"
"pull-request``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:146
msgid "``oe-init-build-env``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:148
msgid ""
"This script sets up the OpenEmbedded build environment. Running this "
"script with the ``source`` command in a shell makes changes to ``PATH`` "
"and sets other core BitBake variables based on the current working "
"directory. You need to run an environment setup script before running "
"BitBake commands. The script uses other scripts within the ``scripts`` "
"directory to do the bulk of the work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:155
msgid ""
"When you run this script, your Yocto Project environment is set up, a "
":term:`Build Directory` is created, your working directory becomes the "
":term:`Build Directory`, and you are presented with some simple "
"suggestions as to what to do next, including a list of some possible "
"targets to build. Here is an example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:177
msgid ""
"When using :doc:`bitbake-setup <bitbake:bitbake-user-manual/bitbake-user-"
"manual-environment-setup>`, this script is indirectly sourced when "
"sourcing the ``init-build-env`` located in the :term:`bitbake:Setup`'s "
"build directory. However, compared to ``oe-init-build-env``, the bitbake-"
"setup ``init-build-env`` does not take any argument, as it is already "
"located in a :term:`Build Directory`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:183
msgid ""
"See :doc:`bitbake:bitbake-user-manual/bitbake-user-manual-environment-"
"setup` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:186
msgid ""
"The default output of the ``oe-init-build-env`` script is from the "
"``conf-summary.txt`` and ``conf-notes.txt`` files, which are found in the"
" ``meta/conf/templates/default`` directory of :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core "
"(OE-Core)`. If you design a custom distribution, you can include your own"
" versions of these configuration files where you can provide a brief "
"summary and detailed usage notes, such as a list of the targets defined "
"by your distribution. See the \":ref:`dev-manual/custom-template-"
"configuration-directory:creating a custom template configuration "
"directory`\" section in the Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual for "
"more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:197
msgid ""
"By default, running this script without a :term:`Build Directory` "
"argument creates the ``build/`` directory in your current working "
"directory. If you provide a :term:`Build Directory` argument when you "
"``source`` the script, you direct the OpenEmbedded build system to create"
" a :term:`Build Directory` of your choice. For example, the following "
"command creates a :term:`Build Directory` named ``mybuilds/`` that is "
"outside of the :term:`Source Directory`::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:207
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system uses the template configuration files, "
"which are found by default in the ``meta/conf/templates/default`` "
"directory in :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)`. See the \":ref:`dev-"
"manual/custom-template-configuration-directory:creating a custom template"
" configuration directory`\" section in the Yocto Project Development "
"Tasks Manual for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:216
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system does not support file or directory names "
"that contain spaces. If you attempt to run the ``oe-init-build-env`` "
"script from a :term:`Source Directory` that contains spaces in either the"
" filenames or directory names, the script returns an error indicating no "
"such file or directory. Be sure to use a :term:`Source Directory` free of"
" names containing spaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:224
msgid "``layers/meta-yocto/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:226
msgid "The ``meta-yocto`` repository is hosted at :yocto_git:`/meta-yocto`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:231
msgid "``meta-poky/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:233
msgid ""
"Dependent on the :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)` metadata, this "
"directory adds just enough metadata to define the Poky reference "
"distribution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:236
msgid ""
"Namely, it contains the distro :term:`configuration file` for Poky: "
"``conf/distro/poky.conf``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:242
msgid "``meta-yocto-bsp/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:244
msgid ""
"This directory contains the Yocto Project reference hardware Board "
"Support Packages (BSPs). For more information on BSPs, see the :doc"
":`/bsp-manual/index`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:251
msgid "The Build Directory --- ``build/``"
msgstr "La Directory di Build --- ``build/``"

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:253
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system creates the :term:`Build Directory` when "
"you run the build environment setup script :ref:`structure-core-script`. "
"If you do not give the :term:`Build Directory` a specific name when you "
"run the setup script, the name defaults to ``build/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:258
msgid ""
"For subsequent parsing and processing, the name of the Build directory is"
" available via the :term:`TOPDIR` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:264
msgid "``build/buildhistory/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:266
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system creates this directory when you enable "
"build history via the :ref:`ref-classes-buildhistory` class file. The "
"directory organizes build information into image, packages, and SDK "
"subdirectories. For information on the build history feature, see the "
"\":ref:`dev-manual/build-quality:maintaining build output quality with "
"\\`\\`buildhistory\\`\\``\" section in the Yocto Project Development "
"Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:276
msgid "``build/cache/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:278
msgid ""
"This directory contains several internal files used by the OpenEmbedded "
"build system. The path to this directory is defined by the "
":term:`PERSISTENT_DIR` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:282
msgid ""
"It also contains ``sanity_info``, a text file keeping track of important "
"build information such as the values of :term:`TMPDIR`, "
":term:`SSTATE_DIR`, as well as the name and version of the host "
"distribution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:286
msgid "This directory should not be shared between builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:290
msgid ""
"The default location of the :ref:`overview-manual/concepts:Hash "
"Equivalence` database is this directory (when :term:`BB_HASHSERVE` is set"
" to ``auto``). If you need to share the :ref:`overview-"
"manual/concepts:Hash Equivalence` database, don't share this file and "
"instead refer to the :doc:`/dev-manual/hashequivserver` document of the "
"Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual to setup a Hash Equivalence "
"server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:300
msgid "``build/conf/local.conf``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:302
msgid ""
"This configuration file contains the local user configurations for the "
"build environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:305
msgid ""
"You could for example experiment with setting (or adding to) "
":term:`DISTRO_FEATURES` or :term:`IMAGE_FEATURES`, or adjust build "
"configurations for specific recipes by setting the :term:`PACKAGECONFIG` "
"variables for them. If you would like to publish and share changes made "
"to this file, it is recommended to put them into a distro "
":term:`configuration file`, or to create layer :term:`configuration "
"fragments <Configuration Fragment>` from changes made here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:313
msgid ""
"The :term:`OpenEmbedded Build System` can create the :ref:`structure-"
"build-conf-local.conf` file from a ``local.conf.sample`` file when you "
"``source`` the top-level build environment setup script :ref:`structure-"
"core-script`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:317
msgid ""
"The source ``local.conf.sample`` file used depends on the "
":term:`TEMPLATECONF` script variable, which defaults to "
":oecore_path:`meta/conf/templates/default` in :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core "
"(OE-Core)`. Because the script variable points to the source of the "
"``local.conf.sample`` file, this implies that you can configure your "
"build environment from any layer by setting the variable in the top-level"
" build environment setup script as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:328
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Once the build process gets the sample file, it uses ``sed`` to "
"substitute final ``${``\\ :term:`OEROOT`\\ ``}`` values for all "
"``##OEROOT##`` values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:335
msgid ""
"You can see how the :term:`TEMPLATECONF` variable is used by looking at "
"the ``layers/openembedded-core/scripts/oe-setup-builddir`` script in "
":term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:341
msgid "``build/conf/bblayers.conf``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:343
msgid ""
"This configuration file defines :ref:`layers <dev-"
"manual/layers:understanding and creating layers>`, which are directory "
"trees, traversed (or walked) by BitBake. The :ref:`structure-build-conf-"
"bblayers.conf` file uses the :term:`BBLAYERS` variable to list the layers"
" BitBake tries to find."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:349
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system can create it from a "
"``bblayers.conf.sample`` file when you ``source`` the top-level build "
"environment setup script (i.e. :ref:`structure-core-script`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:353
msgid ""
"As with the :ref:`structure-build-conf-local.conf` file, the source "
"``bblayers.conf.sample`` file used depends on the :term:`TEMPLATECONF` "
"script variable, which defaults to "
":oecore_path:`meta/conf/templates/default` . Because the script variable "
"points to the source of the ``bblayers.conf.sample`` file, this implies "
"that you can base your build from any layer by setting the variable in "
"the top-level build environment setup script as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:362
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Once the build process gets the sample file, it uses ``sed`` to "
"substitute final ``${``\\ :term:`OEROOT`\\ ``}`` values for all "
"``##OEROOT##`` occurrences."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:367
msgid ""
"You can see how the :term:`TEMPLATECONF` variable is defined by the "
"``scripts/oe-setup-builddir`` script in :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-"
"Core)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:373
msgid "``build/conf/site.conf``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:375
msgid ""
"This configuration file contains the site specific configurations for "
"your build environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:378
msgid ""
"You could for example edit this file to limit the number of threads used "
"by :term:`BitBake` (:term:`BB_NUMBER_THREADS`) or set the location from "
"which you want to access downloaded files (:term:`DL_DIR`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:382
msgid ""
"This file can be shared for multiple build directories. For example, :doc"
":`bitbake-setup <bitbake:bitbake-user-manual/bitbake-user-manual-"
"environment-setup>` makes the :ref:`structure-build-conf-site.conf` file "
"a symbolic link to a common :ref:`structure-build-conf-auto.conf` file::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:397
msgid ""
"This way, site-specific settings can be shared for multiple build "
"configurations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:402
msgid ""
"For more information on how to use ``bitbake-setup``, see the :doc"
":`/brief-yoctoprojectqs/index` document."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:408
msgid "``build/conf/bblock.conf``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:410
msgid ""
"This configuration file is generated by :doc:`bblock </dev-"
"manual/bblock>` and contains the signatures locked by ``bblock``. By "
"default, it does not exist and will be created upon the first invocation "
"of ``bblock``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:417
msgid "``build/conf/toolcfg.conf``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:419
msgid ""
"This file contains configuration variables that are automatically "
"modified by tools such as :oe_git:`bitbake-config-build "
"</bitbake/tree/bin/bitbake-config-build>`. This file should not be "
"modified manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:426
msgid "``build/conf/auto.conf``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:428
msgid ""
"This file is intended for use by continuous integration environments, "
"such as the Yocto Project's :doc:`Autobuilder </test-manual/understand-"
"autobuilder>`. It should not be created or modified when running local "
"builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:435
msgid "``build/downloads/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:437
msgid ""
"This directory contains downloaded upstream source tarballs. You can "
"reuse the directory for multiple builds or move the directory to another "
"location. You can control the location of this directory through the "
":term:`DL_DIR` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:445
msgid "``build/sstate-cache/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:447
msgid ""
"This directory contains the shared state cache. You can reuse the "
"directory for multiple builds or move the directory to another location. "
"You can control the location of this directory through the "
":term:`SSTATE_DIR` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:455
msgid "``build/tmp/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:457
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system creates and uses this directory for all the"
" build system's output. The :term:`TMPDIR` variable points to this "
"directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:461
msgid ""
"BitBake creates this directory if it does not exist. As a last resort, to"
" clean up a build and start it from scratch (other than the downloads), "
"you can remove everything in the ``tmp`` directory or get rid of the "
"directory completely. If you do, you should also completely remove the "
"``build/sstate-cache`` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:470
msgid "``build/tmp/buildstats/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:472
msgid ""
"This directory stores the build statistics as generated by the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-buildstats` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:478
msgid "``build/tmp/cache/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:480
msgid ""
"When BitBake parses the metadata (recipes and configuration files), it "
"caches the results in ``build/tmp/cache/`` to speed up future builds. The"
" results are stored on a per-machine basis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:484
msgid ""
"During subsequent builds, BitBake checks each recipe (together with, for "
"example, any files included or appended to it) to see if they have been "
"modified. Changes can be detected, for example, through file modification"
" time (mtime) changes and hashing of file contents. If no changes to the "
"file are detected, then the parsed result stored in the cache is reused. "
"If the file has changed, it is reparsed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:494
msgid "``build/tmp/deploy/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:496
msgid ""
"This directory contains any \"end result\" output from the OpenEmbedded "
"build process. The :term:`DEPLOY_DIR` variable points to this directory. "
"For more detail on the contents of the ``deploy`` directory, see the "
"\":ref:`overview-manual/concepts:images`\" and \":ref:`overview-"
"manual/concepts:application development sdk`\" sections in the Yocto "
"Project Overview and Concepts Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:507
msgid "``build/tmp/deploy/deb/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:509
msgid ""
"This directory receives any ``.deb`` packages produced by the build "
"process. The packages are sorted into feeds for different architecture "
"types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:516
msgid "``build/tmp/deploy/rpm/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:518
msgid ""
"This directory receives any ``.rpm`` packages produced by the build "
"process. The packages are sorted into feeds for different architecture "
"types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:525
msgid "``build/tmp/deploy/ipk/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:527
msgid "This directory receives ``.ipk`` packages produced by the build process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:532
msgid "``build/tmp/deploy/licenses/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:534
msgid ""
"This directory receives package licensing information. For example, the "
"directory contains sub-directories for ``bash``, ``busybox``, and "
"``glibc`` (among others) that in turn contain appropriate ``COPYING`` "
"license files with other licensing information. For information on "
"licensing, see the \":ref:`dev-manual/licenses:maintaining open source "
"license compliance during your product's lifecycle`\" section in the "
"Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:545
msgid "``build/tmp/deploy/spdx/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:547
msgid ""
"This directory contains JSON files in :term:`SPDX` format which are used "
"to create a Software Bill of Materials (:term:`SBOM`), as explained in "
"detail in the \":ref:`dev-manual/sbom:creating a software bill of "
"materials`\" section of the Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:555
msgid "``build/tmp/deploy/images/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:557
msgid ""
"This directory is populated with the basic output objects of the build "
"(think of them as the \"generated artifacts\" of the build process), "
"including things like the boot loader image, kernel, root filesystem and "
"more. If you want to flash the resulting image from a build onto a "
"device, look here for the necessary components."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:563
msgid ""
"Be careful when deleting files in this directory. You can safely delete "
"old images from this directory (e.g. ``core-image-*``). However, the "
"kernel (``*zImage*``, ``*uImage*``, etc.), bootloader and other "
"supplementary files might be deployed here prior to building an image. "
"Because these files are not directly produced from the image, if you "
"delete them they will not be automatically re-created when you build the "
"image again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:571
msgid ""
"If you do accidentally delete files here, you will need to force them to "
"be re-created. In order to do that, you will need to know the target that"
" produced them. For example, these commands rebuild and re-create the "
"kernel files::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:582
msgid "``build/tmp/deploy/sdk/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:584
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system creates this directory to hold toolchain "
"installer scripts which, when executed, install the sysroot that matches "
"your target hardware. You can find out more about these installers in the"
" \":ref:`sdk-manual/appendix-obtain:building an sdk installer`\" section "
"in the Yocto Project Application Development and the Extensible Software "
"Development Kit (eSDK) manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:594
msgid "``build/tmp/hosttools/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:596
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system uses this directory to create symbolic "
"links to some of the host components that are allowed to be called within"
" tasks. These are basic components listed in the :ref:`ref-manual/system-"
"requirements:required packages for the build host` section. These "
"components are also listed in the :term:`HOSTTOOLS` variable and are "
"limited to this list to prevent host contamination."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:606
msgid "``build/tmp/pkgdata/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:608
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system uses this directory to store package "
"metadata generated during the :ref:`ref-tasks-packagedata` task. The "
"files stored in this directory contain information about each output "
"package produced by the OpenEmbedded build system, and are used in "
"different ways by the build system such as \":ref:`dev-"
"manual/debugging:viewing package information with ``oe-pkgdata-util```\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:618
msgid "``build/tmp/sstate-control/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:620
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system uses this directory for the shared state "
"manifest files. The shared state code uses these files to record the "
"files installed by each sstate task so that the files can be removed when"
" cleaning the recipe or when a newer version is about to be installed. "
"The build system also uses the manifests to detect and produce a warning "
"when files from one task are overwriting those from another."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:631
msgid "``build/tmp/sysroots-components/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:633
msgid ""
"This directory is the location of the sysroot contents that the task :ref"
":`ref-tasks-prepare_recipe_sysroot` links or copies into the recipe-"
"specific sysroot for each recipe listed in :term:`DEPENDS`. Population of"
" this directory is handled through shared state, while the path is "
"specified by the :term:`COMPONENTS_DIR` variable. Apart from a few "
"unusual circumstances, handling of the ``sysroots-components`` directory "
"should be automatic, and recipes should not directly reference "
"``build/tmp/sysroots-components``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:646
msgid "``build/tmp/sysroots/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:648
msgid ""
"Previous versions of the OpenEmbedded build system used to create a "
"global shared sysroot per machine along with a native sysroot. Since the "
"2.3 version of the Yocto Project, there are sysroots in recipe-specific "
":term:`WORKDIR` directories. Thus, the ``build/tmp/sysroots/`` directory "
"is unused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:656
msgid ""
"The ``build/tmp/sysroots/`` directory can still be populated using the "
"``bitbake build-sysroots`` command and can be used for compatibility in "
"some cases. However, in general it is not recommended to populate this "
"directory. Individual recipe-specific sysroots should be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:664
msgid "``build/tmp/stamps/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:666
msgid ""
"This directory holds information that BitBake uses for accounting "
"purposes to track what tasks have run and when they have run. The "
"directory is sub-divided by architecture, package name, and version. Here"
" is an example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:673
msgid ""
"Although the files in the directory are empty of data, BitBake uses the "
"filenames and timestamps for tracking purposes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:676 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10508
msgid ""
"For information on how BitBake uses stamp files to determine if a task "
"should be rerun, see the \":ref:`overview-manual/concepts:stamp files and"
" the rerunning of tasks`\" section in the Yocto Project Overview and "
"Concepts Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:684
msgid "``build/tmp/log/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:686
msgid ""
"This directory contains general logs that are not otherwise placed using "
"the package's :term:`WORKDIR`. Examples of logs are the output from the "
"``do_check_pkg`` or ``do_distro_check`` tasks. Running a build does not "
"necessarily mean this directory is created."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:694
msgid "``build/tmp/work/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:696
msgid ""
"This directory contains architecture-specific work sub-directories for "
"packages built by BitBake. All tasks execute from the appropriate work "
"directory. For example, the source for a particular package is unpacked, "
"patched, configured and compiled all within its own work directory. "
"Within the work directory, organization is based on the package group and"
" version for which the source is being compiled as defined by the "
":term:`WORKDIR`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:704
msgid ""
"It is worth considering the structure of a typical work directory. As an "
"example, consider ``linux-yocto-kernel-3.0`` on the machine ``qemux86`` "
"built within the Yocto Project. For this package, a work directory of "
"``tmp/work/qemux86-poky-linux/linux-yocto/3.0+git1+<.....>``, referred to"
" as the :term:`WORKDIR`, is created. Within this directory, the source is"
" unpacked to ``sources/linux-qemux86-standard-build`` and then patched by"
" Quilt. (See the \":ref:`dev-manual/quilt:using quilt in your workflow`\""
" section in the Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual for more "
"information.) Within the ``linux-qemux86-standard-build`` directory, "
"standard Quilt directories ``linux-3.0/patches`` and ``linux-3.0/.pc`` "
"are created, and standard Quilt commands can be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:716
msgid ""
"There are other directories generated within :term:`WORKDIR`. The most "
"important directory is ``WORKDIR/temp/``, which has log files for each "
"task (``log.do_*.pid``) and contains the scripts BitBake runs for each "
"task (``run.do_*.pid``). The ``WORKDIR/image/`` directory is where \"make"
" install\" places its output that is then split into sub-packages within "
"``WORKDIR/packages-split/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:726
msgid "``build/tmp/work/tunearch/recipename/version/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:728
#, python-brace-format
msgid "The recipe work directory --- ``${WORKDIR}``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:730
msgid ""
"As described earlier in the \":ref:`structure-build-tmp-sysroots`\" "
"section, beginning with the 2.3 release of the Yocto Project, the "
"OpenEmbedded build system builds each recipe in its own work directory "
"(i.e. :term:`WORKDIR`). The path to the work directory is constructed "
"using the architecture of the given build (e.g. :term:`TUNE_PKGARCH`, "
":term:`MACHINE_ARCH`, or \"allarch\"), the recipe name, and the version "
"of the recipe (i.e. :term:`PE`\\ ``:``\\ :term:`PV`\\ ``-``\\ "
":term:`PR`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:740
msgid "Here are key subdirectories within each recipe work directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:742
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``${WORKDIR}/temp``: Contains the log files of each task executed for "
"this recipe, the \"run\" files for each executed task, which contain the "
"code run, and a ``log.task_order`` file, which lists the order in which "
"tasks were executed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:747
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``${WORKDIR}/image``: Contains the output of the :ref:`ref-tasks-install`"
" task, which corresponds to the ``${``\\ :term:`D`\\ ``}`` variable in "
"that task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:751
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``${WORKDIR}/pseudo``: Contains the pseudo database and log for any tasks"
" executed under pseudo for the recipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:754
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``${WORKDIR}/sysroot-destdir``: Contains the output of the :ref:`ref-"
"tasks-populate_sysroot` task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:757
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``${WORKDIR}/package``: Contains the output of the :ref:`ref-tasks-"
"package` task before the output is split into individual packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:761
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``${WORKDIR}/packages-split``: Contains the output of the :ref:`ref-"
"tasks-package` task after the output has been split into individual "
"packages. There are subdirectories for each individual package created by"
" the recipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:766
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``${WORKDIR}/recipe-sysroot``: A directory populated with the target "
"dependencies of the recipe. This directory looks like the target "
"filesystem and contains libraries that the recipe might need to link "
"against (e.g. the C library)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:771
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``${WORKDIR}/recipe-sysroot-native``: A directory populated with the "
"native dependencies of the recipe. This directory contains the tools the "
"recipe needs to build (e.g. the compiler, Autoconf, libtool, and so "
"forth)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:776
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``${WORKDIR}/build``: This subdirectory applies only to recipes that "
"support builds where the source is separate from the build artifacts. The"
" OpenEmbedded build system uses this directory as a separate build "
"directory (i.e. ``${``\\ :term:`B`\\ ``}``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:784
msgid "``build/tmp/work-shared/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:786
msgid ""
"For efficiency, the OpenEmbedded build system creates and uses this "
"directory to hold recipes that share a work directory with other recipes."
" This is for example used for ``gcc`` and its variants (e.g. ``gcc-"
"cross``, ``libgcc``, ``gcc-runtime``, and so forth), or by the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-kernel` class to make the kernel source code and kernel build "
"artifacts available to out-of-tree kernel modules or other kernel-"
"dependent recipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:794
msgid ""
"In practice, only a few recipes make use of the ``work-shared`` "
"directory. This directory is especially useful for recipes that would "
"induce a lot of storage space if they were to be shared with the standard"
" :term:`Sysroot` mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:801
msgid "The OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core) Metadata --- ``meta/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:803
msgid ""
"As mentioned previously, the :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)` "
":term:`metadata` is the core of the Yocto Project. This metadata has "
"several important subdivisions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:810
msgid "``meta/classes*/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:812
msgid ""
"These directories contain the ``*.bbclass`` files. Class files are used "
"to abstract common code so it can be reused by multiple packages. Every "
"package inherits the :ref:`ref-classes-base` file. Examples of other "
"important classes are :ref:`ref-classes-autotools`, which in theory "
"allows any Autotool-enabled package to work with the Yocto Project with "
"minimal effort. Another example is :ref:`ref-classes-kernel` that "
"contains common code and functions for working with the Linux kernel. "
"Functions like image generation or packaging also have their specific "
"class files such as :ref:`ref-classes-image`, :ref:`ref-classes-rootfs*` "
"and :ref:`package*.bbclass <ref-classes-package>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:823
msgid ""
"For reference information on classes, see the \":doc:`/ref-"
"manual/classes`\" chapter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:829
msgid "``meta/conf/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:831
msgid ""
"This directory contains the core set of configuration files that start "
"from ``bitbake.conf`` and from which all other configuration files are "
"included. See the include statements at the end of the ``bitbake.conf`` "
"file and you will note that even :ref:`structure-build-conf-local.conf` "
"is loaded from there. While ``bitbake.conf`` sets up the defaults, you "
"can often override these by using the (:ref:`structure-build-conf-"
"local.conf`) file, machine file or the distribution configuration file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:842
msgid "``meta/conf/machine/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:844
msgid ""
"This directory contains all the machine configuration files. If you set "
"``MACHINE = \"qemux86\"``, the OpenEmbedded build system looks for a "
"``qemux86.conf`` file in this directory. The ``include`` directory "
"contains various data common to multiple machines. If you want to add "
"support for a new machine to the Yocto Project, look in this directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:853
msgid "``meta/conf/distro/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:855
msgid ""
"The contents of this directory controls any distribution-specific "
"configurations. For the Yocto Project, the ``defaultsetup.conf`` is the "
"main file here. This directory includes the versions and the "
":term:`SRCDATE` definitions for applications that are configured here. An"
" example of an alternative configuration might be ``poky-bleeding.conf``."
" Although this file mainly inherits its configuration from Poky."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:865
msgid "``meta/conf/machine-sdk/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:867
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system searches this directory for configuration "
"files that correspond to the value of :term:`SDKMACHINE`. By default, "
"32-bit and 64-bit x86 files ship with the Yocto Project that support some"
" SDK hosts. However, it is possible to extend that support to other SDK "
"hosts by adding additional configuration files in this subdirectory "
"within another layer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:878
msgid "``meta/files/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:880
msgid ""
"This directory contains common license files and several text files used "
"by the build system, as well as :ref:`Wic files <dev-manual/wic:creating "
"partitioned images using wic>` that define the layouts of the images "
"produced by the build. The text files contain minimal device information "
"and lists of files and directories with known permissions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:890
msgid "``meta/lib/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:892
msgid ""
"This directory contains OpenEmbedded Python library code used during the "
"build process. It is enabled via the ``addpylib`` directive in "
"``meta/conf/layer.conf``. For more information, see :ref:`bitbake-user-"
"manual/bitbake-user-manual-metadata:extending python library code`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:900
msgid "``meta/recipes-bsp/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:902
msgid ""
"This directory contains anything linking to specific hardware or hardware"
" configuration information such as \"u-boot\" and \"grub\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:908
msgid "``meta/recipes-connectivity/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:910
msgid ""
"This directory contains libraries and applications related to "
"communication with other devices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:916
msgid "``meta/recipes-core/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:918
msgid ""
"This directory contains what is needed to build a basic working Linux "
"image including commonly used dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:924
msgid "``meta/recipes-devtools/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:926
msgid ""
"This directory contains tools that are primarily used by the build "
"system. The tools, however, can also be used on targets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:932
msgid "``meta/recipes-extended/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:934
msgid ""
"This directory contains non-essential applications that add features "
"compared to the alternatives in core. You might need this directory for "
"full tool functionality."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:941
msgid "``meta/recipes-gnome/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:943
msgid ""
"This directory contains all things related to the GTK+ application "
"framework."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:949
msgid "``meta/recipes-graphics/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:951
msgid "This directory contains X and other graphically related system libraries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:957
msgid "``meta/recipes-kernel/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:959
msgid ""
"This directory contains the kernel and generic applications and libraries"
" that have strong kernel dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:965
msgid "``meta/recipes-multimedia/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:967
msgid ""
"This directory contains codecs and support utilities for audio, images "
"and video."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:973
msgid "``meta/recipes-rt/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:975
msgid ""
"This directory contains package and image recipes for using and testing "
"the ``PREEMPT_RT`` kernel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:981
msgid "``meta/recipes-sato/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:983
msgid ""
"This directory contains the Sato demo/reference UI/UX and its associated "
"applications and configuration data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:989
msgid "``meta/recipes-support/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:991
msgid ""
"This directory contains recipes used by other recipes, but that are not "
"directly included in images (i.e. dependencies of other recipes)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:997
msgid "``meta/site/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:999
msgid ""
"This directory contains a list of cached results for various "
"architectures. Because certain \"autoconf\" test results cannot be "
"determined when cross-compiling due to the tests not able to run on a "
"live system, the information in this directory is passed to \"autoconf\" "
"for the various architectures."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:1008
msgid "``meta/recipes.txt``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/structure.rst:1010
msgid "This file is a description of the contents of ``recipes-*``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:5
msgid "System Requirements"
msgstr "Requisiti di Sistema"

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:7
msgid ""
"Welcome to the Yocto Project Reference Manual. This manual provides "
"reference information for the current release of the Yocto Project, and "
"is most effectively used after you have an understanding of the basics of"
" the Yocto Project. The manual is neither meant to be read as a starting "
"point to the Yocto Project, nor read from start to finish. Rather, use "
"this manual to find variable definitions, class descriptions, and so "
"forth as needed during the course of using the Yocto Project."
msgstr "Benvenuti nel Manuale di Riferimento di Yocto Project. Questo manuale fornisce informazioni di riferimento per la versione corrente di Yocto Project ed è più efficace se si hanno familiarità con i concetti di base del progetto. Il manuale non deve essere letto come punto di partenza per Yocto Project, né dall'inizio alla fine. Piuttosto, va utilizzato per trovare definizioni di variabili, descrizioni di classi e così via, a seconda delle necessità durante l'utilizzo di Yocto Project."

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:16
msgid ""
"For introductory information on the Yocto Project, see the "
":yocto_home:`Yocto Project Website <>` and the \":ref:`overview-manual"
"/development-environment:the yocto project development environment`\" "
"chapter in the Yocto Project Overview and Concepts Manual."
msgstr "Per informazioni introduttive su Yocto Project, consultate il :yocto_home:`Sito Web di Yocto Project <>` e il capitolo \":ref:`overview-manual/development-environment:the yocto project development environment`\" nel Manuale Introduttivo e Concetti di Yocto Project."

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:21
msgid ""
"If you want to use the Yocto Project to quickly build an image without "
"having to understand concepts, work through the :doc:`/brief-"
"yoctoprojectqs/index` document. You can find \"how-to\" information in "
"the :doc:`/dev-manual/index`. You can find Yocto Project overview and "
"conceptual information in the :doc:`/overview-manual/index`."
msgstr "Per utilizzare Yocto Project per creare rapidamente un'immagine senza dover comprendere i concetti, consultate il documento :doc:`/brief-yoctoprojectqs/index`. Le istruzioni pratiche sono disponibili nel file :doc:`/dev-manual/index`. Una panoramica di Yocto Project e informazioni concettuali sono disponibili nel file :doc:`/overview-manual/index`."

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:29
msgid ""
"For more information about the Yocto Project Documentation set, see the "
":ref:`ref-manual/resources:links and related documentation` section."
msgstr "Per ulteriori informazioni sulla documentazione di Yocto Project, consultare la sezione :ref:`ref-manual/resources:links and related documentation`."

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:33
msgid "Minimum Free Disk Space"
msgstr "Spazio Libero Minimo su Disco"

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:35
msgid ""
"To build an image such as ``core-image-sato`` for the ``qemux86-64`` "
"machine, you need a system with at least 140 Gbytes of free disk space. "
"However, much more disk space will be necessary to build more complex "
"images, to run multiple builds and to cache build artifacts, improving "
"build efficiency."
msgstr "Per creare un'immagine come ``core-image-sato`` per la macchina ``qemux86-64``, è necessario un sistema con almeno 140 GB di spazio libero su disco. Tuttavia, sarà necessario molto più spazio su disco per creare immagini più complesse, per eseguire più build e per memorizzare nella cache gli artefatti di build, migliorando l'efficienza del processo."

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:40
msgid ""
"If you have a shortage of disk space, see the \":doc:`/dev-manual/disk-"
"space`\" section of the Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr "Se lo spazio su disco è insufficiente, consultare la sezione \":doc:`/dev-manual/disk-space`\" del Manuale dei Task di Sviluppo."

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:46
msgid "Minimum System RAM"
msgstr "RAM Minima di Sistema"

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:48
msgid ""
"You will manage to build an image such as ``core-image-sato`` for the "
"``qemux86-64`` machine with as little as 32 Gbytes of RAM on an old "
"system with 4 CPU cores, but your builds will be much faster on a system "
"with as much RAM and as many CPU cores as possible."
msgstr "Sarà possibile creare un'immagine come ``core-image-sato`` per la macchina ``qemux86-64`` con soli 32 GB di RAM su un vecchio sistema con 4 core CPU, ma le build saranno molto più veloci su un sistema con la massima quantità di RAM e il maggior numero possibile di core CPU."

#: ../../ref-manual/system-requirements.rst:56
msgid "Supported Linux Distributions"
msgstr "Distribuzioni Linux Supportate"

