msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1581
msgid ""
"An internal variable specifying the special class override that should "
"currently apply (e.g. \"class-target\", \"class-native\", and so forth). "
"The classes that use this variable (e.g. :ref:`ref-classes-native`, :ref"
":`ref-classes-nativesdk`, and so forth) set the variable to appropriate "
"values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1589
msgid ""
":term:`CLASSOVERRIDE` gets its default \"class-target\" value from the "
"``bitbake.conf`` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1592
msgid ""
"As an example, the following override allows you to install extra files, "
"but only when building for the target::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1599
msgid ""
"Here is an example where ``FOO`` is set to \"native\" when building for "
"the build host, and to \"other\" when not building for the build host::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1606
msgid ""
"The underlying mechanism behind :term:`CLASSOVERRIDE` is simply that it "
"is included in the default value of :term:`OVERRIDES`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1609
msgid ":term:`CLEANBROKEN`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1611
msgid ""
"If set to \"1\" within a recipe, :term:`CLEANBROKEN` specifies that the "
"``make clean`` command does not work for the software being built. "
"Consequently, the OpenEmbedded build system will not try to run ``make "
"clean`` during the :ref:`ref-tasks-configure` task, which is the default "
"behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1616
msgid ":term:`COMBINED_FEATURES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1618
msgid ""
"Provides a list of hardware features that are enabled in both "
":term:`MACHINE_FEATURES` and :term:`DISTRO_FEATURES`. This select list of"
" features contains features that make sense to be controlled both at the "
"machine and distribution configuration level. For example, the "
"\"bluetooth\" feature requires hardware support but should also be "
"optional at the distribution level, in case the hardware supports "
"Bluetooth but you do not ever intend to use it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1626
msgid ":term:`COMMERCIAL_AUDIO_PLUGINS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1628
msgid ""
"This variable is specific to the :oe_git:`GStreamer recipes "
"</openembedded-core/tree/meta/recipes-multimedia/gstreamer/>`. It allows "
"to build the GStreamer `\"ugly\" <https://github.com/GStreamer/gst-"
"plugins-ugly>`__ and `\"bad\" <https://github.com/GStreamer/gst-plugins-"
"bad>`__ audio plugins."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1634 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1644
msgid ""
"See the :ref:`dev-manual/licenses:other variables related to commercial "
"licenses` section for usage details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1636
msgid ":term:`COMMERCIAL_VIDEO_PLUGINS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1638
msgid ""
"This variable is specific to the :oe_git:`GStreamer recipes "
"</openembedded-core/tree/meta/recipes-multimedia/gstreamer/>`. It allows "
"to build the GStreamer `\"ugly\" <https://github.com/GStreamer/gst-"
"plugins-ugly>`__ and `\"bad\" <https://github.com/GStreamer/gst-plugins-"
"bad>`__ video plugins."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1646
msgid ":term:`COMMON_LICENSE_DIR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1648
msgid ""
"Points to ``meta/files/common-licenses`` in :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-"
"Core)`, which is where generic license files reside."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1651
msgid ":term:`COMPATIBLE_HOST`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1653
msgid ""
"A regular expression that resolves to one or more hosts (when the recipe "
"is native) or one or more targets (when the recipe is non-native) with "
"which a recipe is compatible. The regular expression is matched against "
":term:`HOST_SYS`. You can use the variable to stop recipes from being "
"built for classes of systems with which the recipes are not compatible. "
"Stopping these builds is particularly useful with kernels. The variable "
"also helps to increase parsing speed since the build system skips parsing"
" recipes not compatible with the current system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1662
msgid ":term:`COMPATIBLE_MACHINE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1664
msgid ""
"A regular expression that resolves to one or more target machines with "
"which a recipe is compatible. The regular expression is matched against "
":term:`MACHINEOVERRIDES`. You can use the variable to stop recipes from "
"being built for machines with which the recipes are not compatible. "
"Stopping these builds is particularly useful with kernels. The variable "
"also helps to increase parsing speed since the build system skips parsing"
" recipes not compatible with the current machine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1673
msgid ""
"If one wants to have a recipe only available for some architectures (here"
" ``aarch64`` and ``mips64``), the following can be used::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1680
msgid ""
"The first line means \"match all machines whose :term:`MACHINEOVERRIDES` "
"contains the empty string\", which will always be none."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1683
msgid ""
"The second is for matching all machines whose :term:`MACHINEOVERRIDES` "
"contains one override which is exactly ``aarch64``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1686
msgid ""
"The third is for matching all machines whose :term:`MACHINEOVERRIDES` "
"contains one override which is exactly ``mips64``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1689
msgid "The same could be achieved with::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1695
msgid ""
"When :term:`COMPATIBLE_MACHINE` is set in a native recipe, the recipe is "
"always skipped. All native recipes must be entirely target independent "
"and should not rely on :term:`MACHINE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1698
msgid ":term:`COMPLEMENTARY_GLOB`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1700
msgid ""
"Defines wildcards to match when installing a list of complementary "
"packages for all the packages explicitly (or implicitly) installed in an "
"image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1704
msgid ""
"The :term:`COMPLEMENTARY_GLOB` variable uses Unix filename pattern "
"matching (`fnmatch <https://docs.python.org/3/library/fnmatch.html"
"#module-fnmatch>`__), which is similar to the Unix style pathname pattern"
" expansion (`glob <https://docs.python.org/3/library/glob.html>`__)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1709
msgid ""
"The resulting list of complementary packages is associated with an item "
"that can be added to :term:`IMAGE_FEATURES`. An example usage of this is "
"the \"dev-pkgs\" item that when added to :term:`IMAGE_FEATURES` will "
"install -dev packages (containing headers and other development files) "
"for every package in the image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1716
msgid ""
"To add a new feature item pointing to a wildcard, use a variable flag to "
"specify the feature item name and use the value to specify the wildcard. "
"Here is an example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1724
msgid ""
"When installing complementary packages, recommends relationships (set via"
" :term:`RRECOMMENDS`) are always ignored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1726
msgid ":term:`COMPONENTS_DIR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1728
msgid ""
"Stores sysroot components provided by each recipe. The OpenEmbedded build"
" system uses :term:`COMPONENTS_DIR` when constructing recipe-specific "
"sysroots for other recipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1732
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The default is \"``${``\\ :term:`STAGING_DIR`\\ ``}-components``.\" (i.e."
" \"``${``\\ :term:`TMPDIR`\\ ``}/sysroots-components``\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1736 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:2787
msgid ":term:`CONF_VERSION`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1738
msgid ""
"Tracks the version of the local configuration file (:ref:`structure-"
"build-conf-local.conf`). The value for :term:`CONF_VERSION` increments "
"each time ``build/conf/`` compatibility changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1741
msgid ":term:`CONFFILES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1743
msgid ""
"Identifies editable or configurable files that are part of a package. If "
"the Package Management System (PMS) is being used to update packages on "
"the target system, it is possible that configuration files you have "
"changed after the original installation and that you now want to remain "
"unchanged are overwritten. In other words, editable files might exist in "
"the package that you do not want reset as part of the package update "
"process. You can use the :term:`CONFFILES` variable to list the files in "
"the package that you wish to prevent the PMS from overwriting during this"
" update process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1753
msgid ""
"To use the :term:`CONFFILES` variable, provide a package name override "
"that identifies the resulting package. Then, provide a space-separated "
"list of files. Here is an example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1760
msgid ""
"There is a relationship between the :term:`CONFFILES` and :term:`FILES` "
"variables. The files listed within :term:`CONFFILES` must be a subset of "
"the files listed within :term:`FILES`. Because the configuration files "
"you provide with :term:`CONFFILES` are simply being identified so that "
"the PMS will not overwrite them, it makes sense that the files must "
"already be included as part of the package through the :term:`FILES` "
"variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1770
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"When specifying paths as part of the :term:`CONFFILES` variable, it is "
"good practice to use appropriate path variables. For example, "
"``${sysconfdir}`` rather than ``/etc`` or ``${bindir}`` rather than "
"``/usr/bin``. You can find a list of these variables at the top of the "
"``meta/conf/bitbake.conf`` file in :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1776
msgid ":term:`CONFIG_INITRAMFS_SOURCE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1778
msgid ""
"Identifies the initial RAM filesystem (:term:`Initramfs`) source files. "
"The OpenEmbedded build system receives and uses this kernel Kconfig "
"variable as an environment variable. By default, the variable is set to "
"null (\"\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1783
msgid ""
"The :term:`CONFIG_INITRAMFS_SOURCE` can be either a single cpio archive "
"with a ``.cpio`` suffix or a space-separated list of directories and "
"files for building the :term:`Initramfs` image. A cpio archive should "
"contain a filesystem archive to be used as an :term:`Initramfs` image. "
"Directories should contain a filesystem layout to be included in the "
":term:`Initramfs` image. Files should contain entries according to the "
"format described by the ``usr/gen_init_cpio`` program in the kernel tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1791
msgid ""
"If you specify multiple directories and files, the :term:`Initramfs` "
"image will be the aggregate of all of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1794
msgid ""
"For information on creating an :term:`Initramfs`, see the \":ref:`dev-"
"manual/building:building an initial ram filesystem (Initramfs) image`\" "
"section in the Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1797
msgid ":term:`CONFIG_SITE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:1799
msgid ""
"A list of files that contains ``autoconf`` test results relevant to the "
"current build. This variable is used by the Autotools utilities when "
"running ``configure``."
msgstr ""

