#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3347
msgid ":term:`FILESYSTEM_PERMS_TABLES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3349
msgid ""
"Allows you to define your own file permissions settings tables as part of"
" your configuration for the packaging process. For example, suppose you "
"need a consistent set of custom permissions for a set of groups and users"
" across an entire work project. It is best to do this in the packages "
"themselves but this is not always possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3355
msgid ""
"By default, the OpenEmbedded build system uses the ``fs-perms.txt``, "
"``fs-perms-volatile-log.txt`` and ``fs-perms-volatile-tmp.txt`` which are"
" located in the ``meta/files`` folder in :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-"
"Core)`. If you create your own permission setting table files, you should"
" place those in your layer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3361
msgid ""
"You can override the value of :term:`FILESYSTEM_PERMS_TABLES` variable in"
" your distribution configuration file to point to your custom permission "
"table files. You can specify one or more file permissions setting tables."
" The paths that you specify to these files must be defined within the "
":term:`BBPATH` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3367
msgid ""
"In order to disable the volatile log, which is enabled by default, one "
"can remove the ``files/fs-perms-volatile-log.txt`` value from "
"``FILESYSTEM_PERMS_TABLES``. Similarly, in order to disable the volatile "
"tmp, one can remove the ``files/fs-perms-volatile-tmp.txt`` value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3372
msgid ""
"For guidance on how to define your own file permissions settings tables, "
"examine the existing ``fs-perms.txt``, ``fs-perms-volatile-log.txt`` and "
"``fs-perms-volatile-tmp.txt`` files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3375
msgid ":term:`FIRMWARE_COMPRESSION`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3377
msgid ""
"The :term:`FIRMWARE_COMPRESSION` allows compressing the firmware provided"
" by the ``linux-firmware`` recipe. The default value of this variable is "
"an empty string (no compression), and the possible values it can take are"
" ``xz`` and ``zst``. This can allow significant disk space savings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3382
msgid ""
"For this to work, the Linux Kernel requires the "
"``CONFIG_FW_LOADER_COMPRESS_XZ`` or ``CONFIG_FW_LOADER_COMPRESS_ZSTD`` "
"configuration options to be set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3385
msgid ":term:`FIT_ADDRESS_CELLS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3387
msgid ""
"Specifies the value of the ``#address-cells`` value for the description "
"of the FIT image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3390
msgid ""
"The default value is set to \"1\" by the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-fit-"
"image` class, which corresponds to 32 bit addresses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:3393
msgid ""
"For platforms that need to set 64 bit addresses, for example in "
":term:`UBOOT_LOADADDRESS` and :term:`UBOOT_ENTRYPOINT`, you need to set "
"this value to \"2\", as two 32 bit values (cells) will be needed to "
"represent such addresses."
msgstr ""

