msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4868
msgid ""
"The default value of the variable is set as follows in the "
"``meta/conf/distro/defaultsetup.conf`` file::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4872
msgid ":term:`INHIBIT_AUTOTOOLS_DEPS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4874
msgid ""
"Prevents the :ref:`ref-classes-autotools` class from automatically adding"
" its default build-time dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4877
msgid ""
"When a recipe inherits the :ref:`ref-classes-autotools` class, several "
"native cross tools such as ``autoconf-native``, ``automake-native``, "
"``libtool-native``, ``libtool-cross`` are added to :term:`DEPENDS` to "
"support the ``autotools`` build process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4882
msgid ""
"To prevent the build system from adding these dependencies automatically,"
" set the :term:`INHIBIT_AUTOTOOLS_DEPS` variable as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4887
msgid ""
"By default, the value of :term:`INHIBIT_AUTOTOOLS_DEPS` is empty. Setting"
" it to \"0\" does not disable inhibition. Only the empty string will "
"disable inhibition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4890
msgid ":term:`INHIBIT_DEFAULT_DEPS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4892
msgid ""
"Prevents the default dependencies, namely the C compiler and standard C "
"library (libc), from being added to :term:`DEPENDS`. This variable is "
"usually used within recipes that do not require any compilation using the"
" C compiler."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4897
msgid ""
"Set the variable to \"1\" to prevent the default dependencies from being "
"added."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4899
msgid ":term:`INHIBIT_DEFAULT_RUST_DEPS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4901
msgid ""
"Prevents the :ref:`ref-classes-rust` class from automatically adding its "
"default build-time dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4904
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"When a recipe inherits the :ref:`ref-classes-rust` class, several tools "
"such as ``rust-native`` and ``${RUSTLIB_DEP}`` (only added when cross-"
"compiling) are added to :term:`DEPENDS` to support the ``rust`` build "
"process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4908
msgid ""
"To prevent the build system from adding these dependencies automatically,"
" set the :term:`INHIBIT_DEFAULT_RUST_DEPS` variable as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4913
msgid ""
"By default, the value of :term:`INHIBIT_DEFAULT_RUST_DEPS` is empty. "
"Setting it to \"0\" does not disable inhibition. Only the empty string "
"will disable inhibition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4916
msgid ":term:`INHIBIT_PACKAGE_DEBUG_SPLIT`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4918
msgid ""
"Prevents the OpenEmbedded build system from splitting out debug "
"information during packaging. By default, the build system splits out "
"debugging information during the :ref:`ref-tasks-package` task. For more "
"information on how debug information is split out, see the "
":term:`PACKAGE_DEBUG_SPLIT_STYLE` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4926
msgid ""
"To prevent the build system from splitting out debug information during "
"packaging, set the :term:`INHIBIT_PACKAGE_DEBUG_SPLIT` variable as "
"follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4931
msgid ":term:`INHIBIT_PACKAGE_STRIP`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4933
msgid ""
"If set to \"1\", causes the build to not strip binaries in resulting "
"packages and prevents the ``-dbg`` package from containing the source "
"files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4937
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"By default, the OpenEmbedded build system strips binaries and puts the "
"debugging symbols into ``${``\\ :term:`PN`\\ ``}-dbg``. Consequently, you"
" should not set :term:`INHIBIT_PACKAGE_STRIP` when you plan to debug in "
"general."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4941
msgid ":term:`INHIBIT_SYSROOT_STRIP`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4943
msgid ""
"If set to \"1\", causes the build to not strip binaries in the resulting "
"sysroot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4946
msgid ""
"By default, the OpenEmbedded build system strips binaries in the "
"resulting sysroot. When you specifically set the "
":term:`INHIBIT_SYSROOT_STRIP` variable to \"1\" in your recipe, you "
"inhibit this stripping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4951
msgid ""
"If you want to use this variable, include the :ref:`ref-classes-staging` "
"class. This class uses a ``sys_strip()`` function to test for the "
"variable and acts accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4957
msgid ""
"Use of the :term:`INHIBIT_SYSROOT_STRIP` variable occurs in rare and "
"special circumstances. For example, suppose you are building bare-metal "
"firmware by using an external GCC toolchain. Furthermore, even if the "
"toolchain's binaries are strippable, there are other files needed for the"
" build that are not strippable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4962
msgid ":term:`INHIBIT_UPDATERCD_BBCLASS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4964
msgid ""
"Prevents the :ref:`ref-classes-update-rc.d` class from automatically "
"installing and registering SysV init scripts for packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4967
msgid ""
"When a recipe inherits the :ref:`ref-classes-update-rc.d` class, init "
"scripts are typically installed and registered for the packages listed in"
" :term:`INITSCRIPT_PACKAGES`. This ensures that the relevant services are"
" started and stopped at the appropriate runlevels using the traditional "
"SysV init system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4973
msgid ""
"To prevent the build system from adding these scripts and configurations "
"automatically, set the :term:`INHIBIT_UPDATERCD_BBCLASS` variable as "
"follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4978
msgid ""
"By default, the value of :term:`INHIBIT_UPDATERCD_BBCLASS` is empty. "
"Setting it to \"0\" does not disable inhibition. Only the empty string "
"will disable inhibition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4981
msgid ":term:`INIT_MANAGER`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4983
msgid "Specifies the system init manager to use. Available options are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4985
msgid "``sysvinit``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4987
msgid "``mdev-busybox``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4989
msgid ""
"With ``sysvinit``, the init manager is set to :wikipedia:`SysVinit <Init"
"#SysV-style>`, the traditional UNIX init system. This is the default "
"choice in the :term:`Poky` distribution, together with the Udev device "
"manager (see the \":ref:`device-manager`\" section)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4994
msgid ""
"With ``systemd``, the init manager becomes :wikipedia:`systemd "
"<Systemd>`, which comes with the :wikipedia:`udev <Udev>` device manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:4997
msgid ""
"With ``mdev-busybox``, the init manager becomes the much simpler BusyBox "
"init, together with the BusyBox mdev device manager. This is the simplest"
" and lightest solution, and probably the best choice for low-end systems "
"with a rather slow CPU and a limited amount of RAM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5002
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"More concretely, this is used to include ``conf/distro/include/init-"
"manager-${INIT_MANAGER}.inc`` into the global configuration. You can have"
" a look at the :oe_git:`meta/conf/distro/include/init-manager-*.inc "
"</openembedded-core/tree/meta/conf/distro/include>` files for more "
"information, and also the \":ref:`init-manager`\" section in the Yocto "
"Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5008
msgid ":term:`INITRAMFS_DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5010
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Indicates the deploy directory used by :ref:`ref-tasks-bundle_initramfs` "
"where the :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` will be fetched from. This variable is "
"set by default to ``${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}`` in the :ref:`ref-classes-"
"kernel` class and it's only meant to be changed when building an "
":term:`Initramfs` image from a separate multiconfig via "
":term:`INITRAMFS_MULTICONFIG`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5016
msgid ":term:`INITRAMFS_FSTYPES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5018
msgid ""
"Defines the format for the output image of an initial RAM filesystem "
"(:term:`Initramfs`), which is used during boot. Supported formats are the"
" same as those supported by the :term:`IMAGE_FSTYPES` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5023
msgid ""
"The default value of this variable, which is set in the "
"``meta/conf/bitbake.conf`` configuration file in :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core"
" (OE-Core)`, is \"cpio.gz\". The Linux kernel's :term:`Initramfs` "
"mechanism, as opposed to the initial RAM filesystem :wikipedia:`initrd "
"<Initrd>` mechanism, expects an optionally compressed cpio archive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5029
msgid ":term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5031
msgid ""
"Specifies the :term:`PROVIDES` name of an image recipe that is used to "
"build an initial RAM filesystem (:term:`Initramfs`) image. In other "
"words, the :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` variable causes an additional recipe "
"to be built as a dependency to whatever root filesystem recipe you might "
"be using (e.g. ``core-image-sato``). The :term:`Initramfs` image recipe "
"you provide should set :term:`IMAGE_FSTYPES` to "
":term:`INITRAMFS_FSTYPES`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5040
msgid ""
"An :term:`Initramfs` image provides a temporary root filesystem used for "
"early system initialization (e.g. loading of modules needed to locate and"
" mount the \"real\" root filesystem)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5046
msgid ""
"See the ``meta/recipes-core/images/core-image-minimal-initramfs.bb`` "
"recipe in :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)` for an example "
":term:`Initramfs` recipe. To select this sample recipe as the one built "
"to provide the :term:`Initramfs` image, set :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` to "
"\"core-image-minimal-initramfs\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5052
msgid ""
"You can also find more information by referencing the "
":oecore_path:`meta/conf/templates/default/local.conf.sample.extended` "
"configuration file in :term:`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)`, the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-image` class, and the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel` class to see how "
"to use the :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5058
msgid ""
"If :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` is empty, which is the default, then no "
":term:`Initramfs` image is built."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5061
msgid ""
"For more information, you can also see the :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE_BUNDLE`"
" variable, which allows the generated image to be bundled inside the "
"kernel image. Additionally, for information on creating an "
":term:`Initramfs` image, see the \":ref:`dev-manual/building:building an "
"initial ram filesystem (Initramfs) image`\" section in the Yocto Project "
"Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5067
msgid ":term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE_BUNDLE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5069
msgid ""
"Controls whether or not the image recipe specified by "
":term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` is run through an extra pass (:ref:`ref-tasks-"
"bundle_initramfs`) during kernel compilation in order to build a single "
"binary that contains both the kernel image and the initial RAM filesystem"
" (:term:`Initramfs`) image. This makes use of the "
":term:`CONFIG_INITRAMFS_SOURCE` kernel feature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5081
msgid ""
"Bundling the :term:`Initramfs` with the kernel conflates the code in the "
":term:`Initramfs` with the GPLv2 licensed Linux kernel binary. Thus only "
"GPLv2 compatible software may be part of a bundled :term:`Initramfs`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5087
msgid ""
"Using an extra compilation pass to bundle the :term:`Initramfs` avoids a "
"circular dependency between the kernel recipe and the :term:`Initramfs` "
"recipe should the :term:`Initramfs` include kernel modules. Should that "
"be the case, the :term:`Initramfs` recipe depends on the kernel for the "
"kernel modules, and the kernel depends on the :term:`Initramfs` recipe "
"since the :term:`Initramfs` is bundled inside the kernel image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5094
msgid ""
"The combined binary is deposited into the ``tmp/deploy`` directory, which"
" is part of the :term:`Build Directory`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5097
msgid ""
"Setting the variable to \"1\" in a configuration file causes the "
"OpenEmbedded build system to generate a kernel image with the "
":term:`Initramfs` specified in :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` bundled within::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5103
msgid ""
"By default, the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel` class sets this variable to a "
"null string as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5110
msgid ""
"You must set the :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE_BUNDLE` variable in a "
"configuration file. You cannot set the variable in a recipe file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5113
msgid ""
"See the :oecore_path:`local.conf.sample.extended "
"<meta/conf/templates/default/local.conf.sample.extended>` file for "
"additional information. Also, for information on creating an "
":term:`Initramfs`, see the \":ref:`dev-manual/building:building an "
"initial ram filesystem (Initramfs) image`\" section in the Yocto Project "
"Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5118
msgid ":term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5121
msgid ""
"This value needs to stay in sync with :term:`IMAGE_LINK_NAME`, but with "
":term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` instead of :term:`IMAGE_BASENAME`. The default "
"value is set as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5125
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"INITRAMFS_IMAGE_NAME ?= "
"\"${@['${INITRAMFS_IMAGE}${IMAGE_MACHINE_SUFFIX}', "
"''][d.getVar('INITRAMFS_IMAGE') == '']}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5127
msgid ""
"That is, if :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` is set, the value of "
":term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE_NAME` will be set based upon "
":term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` and :term:`IMAGE_MACHINE_SUFFIX`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5131
msgid ":term:`INITRAMFS_LINK_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5133
msgid ""
"The link name of the initial RAM filesystem image. This variable is set "
"in the ``meta/classes-recipe/kernel-artifact-names.bbclass`` file as "
"follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5139 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5512
#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5592 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5616
#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:6521
msgid ""
"The value of the ``KERNEL_ARTIFACT_LINK_NAME`` variable, which is set in "
"the same file, has the following value::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5145 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5518
#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5598 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5622
#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:6527
msgid "See the :term:`MACHINE` variable for additional information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5147
msgid ":term:`INITRAMFS_MAXSIZE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5149
msgid ""
"Defines the maximum allowed size of the :term:`Initramfs` image in "
"Kbytes. The build will fail if the :term:`Initramfs` image size exceeds "
"this value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5152
msgid ""
"The :term:`Initramfs` image size undergoes several calculation steps "
"before being compared to :term:`INITRAMFS_MAXSIZE`. These steps are the "
"same as those used for :term:`IMAGE_ROOTFS_MAXSIZE` and are described in "
"detail in that entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5157
msgid ""
"Thus, :term:`INITRAMFS_MAXSIZE` is compared with the result of the "
"calculations and is independent of the final image type (e.g. "
"compressed). A default value for :term:`INITRAMFS_MAXSIZE` is set in "
":oe_git:`meta/conf/bitbake.conf </openembedded-"
"core/tree/meta/conf/bitbake.conf>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5161
msgid ":term:`INITRAMFS_MULTICONFIG`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5163
msgid ""
"Defines the multiconfig to create a multiconfig dependency to be used by "
"the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5166
msgid ""
"This allows the kernel to bundle an :term:`INITRAMFS_IMAGE` coming from a"
" separate multiconfig, this is meant to be used in addition to "
":term:`INITRAMFS_DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5169
msgid ""
"For more information on how to bundle an :term:`Initramfs` image from a "
"separate multiconfig see the \":ref:`dev-manual/building:Bundling an "
"Initramfs Image From a Separate Multiconfig`\" section in the Yocto "
"Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5172
msgid ":term:`INITRAMFS_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5174
msgid ""
"The base name of the initial RAM filesystem image. This variable is set "
"in the ``meta/classes-recipe/kernel-artifact-names.bbclass`` file as "
"follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5180 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5528
#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5608 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5645
#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:6535
msgid "See :term:`KERNEL_ARTIFACT_NAME` for additional information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5181
msgid ":term:`INITRD`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5183
msgid ""
"Indicates list of filesystem images to concatenate and use as an initial "
"RAM disk (``initrd``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5186
msgid ""
"The :term:`INITRD` variable is an optional variable used with the :ref"
":`ref-classes-image-live` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5188
msgid ":term:`INITRD_IMAGE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5190
msgid ""
"When building a \"live\" bootable image (i.e. when :term:`IMAGE_FSTYPES` "
"contains \"live\"), :term:`INITRD_IMAGE` specifies the image recipe that "
"should be built to provide the initial RAM disk image. The default value "
"is \"core-image-minimal-initramfs\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5196
msgid "See the :ref:`ref-classes-image-live` class for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5197
msgid ":term:`INITSCRIPT_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5199
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The filename of the initialization script as installed to "
"``${sysconfdir}/init.d``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5202
msgid ""
"This variable is used in recipes when using :ref:`ref-classes-update-"
"rc.d`. The variable is mandatory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5204
msgid ":term:`INITSCRIPT_PACKAGES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5206
msgid ""
"A list of the packages that contain initscripts. If multiple packages are"
" specified, you need to append the package name to the other "
"``INITSCRIPT_*`` as an override."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5210
msgid ""
"This variable is used in recipes when using :ref:`ref-classes-update-"
"rc.d`. The variable is optional and defaults to the :term:`PN` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5213
msgid ":term:`INITSCRIPT_PARAMS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5215
msgid "Specifies the options to pass to ``update-rc.d``. Here is an example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5219
msgid ""
"In this example, the script has a runlevel of 99, starts the script in "
"initlevels 2 and 5, and stops the script in levels 0, 1 and 6."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5222
msgid ""
"The variable's default value is \"defaults\", which is set in the :ref"
":`ref-classes-update-rc.d` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5225
msgid ""
"The value in :term:`INITSCRIPT_PARAMS` is passed through to the ``update-"
"rc.d`` command. For more information on valid parameters, please see the "
"manual page: :manpage:`update-rc.d <update-rc.d(8)>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5228
msgid ":term:`INSANE_SKIP`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5230
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Specifies the QA checks to skip for a specific package within a recipe. "
"For example, to skip the check for symbolic link ``.so`` files in the "
"main package of a recipe, add the following to the recipe. The package "
"name override must be used, which in this example is ``${PN}``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5238
msgid ""
"See the \":ref:`ref-classes-insane`\" section for a list of the valid QA "
"checks you can specify using this variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5240
msgid ":term:`INSTALL_TIMEZONE_FILE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5242
msgid ""
"By default, the ``tzdata`` recipe packages an ``/etc/timezone`` file. Set"
" the :term:`INSTALL_TIMEZONE_FILE` variable to \"0\" at the configuration"
" level to disable this behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5245
msgid ":term:`IPK_FEED_URIS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5247
msgid ""
"When the IPK backend is in use and package management is enabled on the "
"target, you can use this variable to set up ``opkg`` in the target image "
"to point to package feeds on a nominated server. Once the feed is "
"established, you can perform installations or upgrades using the package "
"manager at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5252
msgid ":term:`KARCH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5254
msgid ""
"Defines the kernel architecture used when assembling the configuration. "
"Architectures supported for this release are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5258
msgid "i386"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5261
msgid "qemu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5264
msgid ""
"You define the :term:`KARCH` variable in the :ref:`kernel-"
"dev/advanced:bsp descriptions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5265
msgid ":term:`KBRANCH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5267
msgid ""
"A regular expression used by the build process to explicitly identify the"
" kernel branch that is validated, patched, and configured during a build."
" You must set this variable to ensure the exact kernel branch you want is"
" being used by the build process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5272
msgid ""
"Values for this variable are set in the kernel's recipe file and the "
"kernel's append file. For example, if you are using the ``linux-"
"yocto_4.12`` kernel, the kernel recipe file is the ``meta/recipes-"
"kernel/linux/linux-yocto_4.12.bb`` file. :term:`KBRANCH` is set as "
"follows in that kernel recipe file::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5280
msgid ""
"This variable is also used from the kernel's append file to identify the "
"kernel branch specific to a particular machine or target hardware. "
"Continuing with the previous kernel example, the kernel's append file is "
"located in the BSP layer for a given machine. For example, the append "
"file for the Beaglebone and generic versions of both 32 and 64-bit IA "
"machines (``meta-yocto-bsp``) is named ``meta-yocto-bsp/recipes-"
"kernel/linux/linux-yocto_6.1.bbappend``. Here are the related statements "
"from that append file::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5294
msgid ""
"The :term:`KBRANCH` statements identify the kernel branch to use when "
"building for each supported BSP."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5297
msgid ":term:`KBUILD_DEFCONFIG`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5299
msgid ""
"When used with the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-yocto` class, specifies an "
"\"in-tree\" kernel configuration file for use during a kernel build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5303
msgid ""
"Typically, when using a ``defconfig`` to configure a kernel during a "
"build, you place the file in your layer in the same manner as you would "
"place patch files and configuration fragment files (i.e. \"out-of-"
"tree\"). However, if you want to use a ``defconfig`` file that is part of"
" the kernel tree (i.e. \"in-tree\"), you can use the "
