msgid ":term:`KERNEL_IMAGE_MAXSIZE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5626
msgid ""
"Specifies the maximum allowable size of the kernel image file in "
"kibibytes. If this variable is set, the sizes of all of the kernel image "
"files listed in :term:`KERNEL_IMAGETYPES` are checked against this value "
"during the :ref:`ref-tasks-sizecheck` task. That task will warn about any"
" of the kernel images that exceed the maximum, and will fail only if all "
"images are too large."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5633
msgid ""
":term:`KERNEL_IMAGE_MAXSIZE` is useful for target devices that have a "
"limited amount of space in which the kernel image must be stored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5636
msgid ""
"By default, this variable is not set, which means the size of the kernel "
"images are not checked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5638
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_IMAGE_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5640
msgid ""
"The base name of the kernel image. This variable is set in the ``meta"
"/classes-recipe/kernel-artifact-names.bbclass`` file as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5646
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_IMAGE_STRIP_EXTRA_SECTIONS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5648
msgid ""
"If this variable is set, it should contain the sections to be stripped "
"from the ``vmlinux`` image by the kernel-related :ref:`ref-tasks-strip` "
"task. As a simple example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5653
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_IMAGETYPE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5655
msgid ""
"The type of kernel to build for a device, usually set by the machine "
"configuration files and defaults to \"zImage\". This variable is used "
"when building the kernel and is passed to ``make`` as the target to "
"build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5660
msgid "To build additional kernel image types, use :term:`KERNEL_IMAGETYPES`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5661
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_IMAGETYPES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5663
msgid ""
"Lists additional types of kernel images to build for a device in addition"
" to image type specified in :term:`KERNEL_IMAGETYPE`. Usually set by the "
"machine configuration files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5666
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_LOCALVERSION`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5668
msgid ""
"This variable allows to append a string to the version of the kernel "
"image. This corresponds to the ``CONFIG_LOCALVERSION`` kernel "
"configuration parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5672
msgid ""
"Using this variable is only useful when you are using a kernel recipe "
"inheriting the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel` class, and which doesn't already"
" set a local version. Therefore, setting this variable has no impact on "
"``linux-yocto`` kernels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5676
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_MODULE_AUTOLOAD`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5678
msgid "Lists kernel modules that need to be auto-loaded during boot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5682
msgid "This variable replaces the deprecated :term:`module_autoload` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5685
msgid ""
"You can use the :term:`KERNEL_MODULE_AUTOLOAD` variable anywhere that it "
"can be recognized by the kernel recipe or by an out-of-tree kernel module"
" recipe (e.g. a machine configuration file, a distribution configuration "
"file, an append file for the recipe, or the recipe itself)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5691
msgid "Specify it as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5695
msgid ""
"Including :term:`KERNEL_MODULE_AUTOLOAD` causes the OpenEmbedded build "
"system to populate the ``/etc/modules-load.d/modname.conf`` file with the"
" list of modules to be auto-loaded on boot. The modules appear one-per-"
"line in the file. Here is an example of the most common use case::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5703
msgid ""
"For information on how to populate the ``modname.conf`` file with "
"``modprobe.d`` syntax lines, see the :term:`KERNEL_MODULE_PROBECONF` "
"variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5705
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_MODULE_INSTALL_PREFIX`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5707
msgid ""
"When inheriting the :ref:`ref-classes-module` class, the "
":term:`KERNEL_MODULE_INSTALL_PREFIX` variable specifies the directory in "
"which kernel modules are installed on target."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5710
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_MODULE_PROBECONF`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5712
msgid ""
"Provides a list of modules for which the OpenEmbedded build system "
"expects to find ``module_conf_``\\ modname values that specify "
"configuration for each of the modules. For information on how to provide "
"those module configurations, see the :term:`module_conf_* <module_conf>` "
"variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5717
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_PACKAGE_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5719
msgid ""
"Specifies the base name of the kernel packages, such as \"kernel\" in the"
" kernel packages such as \"kernel-modules\", \"kernel-image\" and "
"\"kernel-dbg\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5723
msgid ""
"The default value for this variable is set to \"kernel\" by the :ref"
":`ref-classes-kernel` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5725
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_PATH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5727 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5761
msgid ""
"The location of the kernel sources. This variable is set to the value of "
"the :term:`STAGING_KERNEL_DIR` within the :ref:`ref-classes-module` "
"class. For information on how this variable is used, see the \":ref"
":`kernel-dev/common:incorporating out-of-tree modules`\" section in the "
"Yocto Project Linux Kernel Development Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5733
msgid ""
"To help maximize compatibility with out-of-tree drivers used to build "
"modules, the OpenEmbedded build system also recognizes and uses the "
":term:`KERNEL_SRC` variable, which is identical to the "
":term:`KERNEL_PATH` variable. Both variables are common variables used by"
" external Makefiles to point to the kernel source directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5738
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_SPLIT_MODULES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5740
msgid ""
"When inheriting the :ref:`ref-classes-kernel-module-split` class, this "
"variable controls whether kernel modules are split into separate packages"
" or bundled into a single package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5744
msgid ""
"For some use cases, a monolithic kernel module package "
":term:`KERNEL_PACKAGE_NAME` that contains all modules built from the "
"kernel sources may be preferred to speed up the installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5748
msgid ""
"By default, this variable is set to ``1``, resulting in one package per "
"module. Setting it to any other value will generate a single monolithic "
"package containing all kernel modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5754
msgid ""
"If :term:`KERNEL_SPLIT_MODULES` is set to 0, it is still possible to "
"install all kernel modules at once by adding ``kernel-modules`` (assuming"
" :term:`KERNEL_PACKAGE_NAME` is ``kernel-modules``) to "
":term:`IMAGE_INSTALL`. The way it works is that a placeholder \"kernel-"
"modules\" package will be created and will depend on every other "
"individual kernel module packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5759
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_SRC`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5767
msgid ""
"To help maximize compatibility with out-of-tree drivers used to build "
"modules, the OpenEmbedded build system also recognizes and uses the "
":term:`KERNEL_PATH` variable, which is identical to the "
":term:`KERNEL_SRC` variable. Both variables are common variables used by "
"external Makefiles to point to the kernel source directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5772
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_STRIP`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5774
msgid ""
"Allows to specific which ``strip`` command to use to strip the kernel "
"binary, typically either GNU binutils ``strip`` or ``llvm-strip``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5776
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_TOOLCHAIN`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5778
msgid ""
"The :term:`KERNEL_TOOLCHAIN` variable allows specifying the toolchain to "
"use to build the Linux kernel. Its value can be one of the values "
"supported by the :term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_TARGET` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5781
msgid ":term:`KERNEL_VERSION`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5783
msgid ""
"Specifies the version of the kernel as extracted from ``version.h`` or "
"``utsrelease.h`` within the kernel sources. Effects of setting this "
"variable do not take effect until the kernel has been configured. "
"Consequently, attempting to refer to this variable in contexts prior to "
"configuration will not work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5788
msgid ":term:`KERNELDEPMODDEPEND`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5790
msgid ""
"Specifies whether the data referenced through :term:`PKGDATA_DIR` is "
"needed or not. :term:`KERNELDEPMODDEPEND` does not control whether or not"
" that data exists, but simply whether or not it is used. If you do not "
"need to use the data, set the :term:`KERNELDEPMODDEPEND` variable in your"
" :term:`Initramfs` recipe. Setting the variable there when the data is "
"not needed avoids a potential dependency loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5797
msgid ":term:`KFEATURE_DESCRIPTION`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5799
msgid ""
"Provides a short description of a configuration fragment. You use this "
"variable in the ``.scc`` file that describes a configuration fragment "
"file. Here is the variable used in a file named ``smp.scc`` to describe "
"SMP being enabled::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5805
msgid ":term:`KMACHINE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:5807
msgid ""
"The machine as known by the kernel. Sometimes the machine name used by "
