#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7380 ../../ref-manual/varlocality.rst:164
msgid ":term:`PACKAGECONFIG_CONFARGS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7382
msgid ""
"A space-separated list of configuration options generated from the "
":term:`PACKAGECONFIG` setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7385
msgid ""
"Classes such as :ref:`ref-classes-autotools` and :ref:`ref-classes-cmake`"
" use :term:`PACKAGECONFIG_CONFARGS` to pass :term:`PACKAGECONFIG` options"
" to ``configure`` and ``cmake``, respectively. If you are using "
":term:`PACKAGECONFIG` but not a class that handles the :ref:`ref-tasks-"
"configure` task, then you need to use :term:`PACKAGECONFIG_CONFARGS` "
"appropriately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7391
msgid ":term:`PACKAGEFUNCS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7393
msgid ""
"The :term:`PACKAGEFUNCS` variable holds a list of functions which are "
"executed to process metadata based on split packages found in the "
":term:`PKGDEST` directory. These functions are executed in the :ref:`ref-"
"classes-package` class in the order found in the list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7397
msgid ":term:`PACKAGEGROUP_DISABLE_COMPLEMENTARY`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7399
msgid ""
"For recipes inheriting the :ref:`ref-classes-packagegroup` class, setting"
" :term:`PACKAGEGROUP_DISABLE_COMPLEMENTARY` to \"1\" specifies that the "
"normal complementary packages (i.e. ``-dev``, ``-dbg``, and so forth) "
"should not be automatically created by the ``packagegroup`` recipe, which"
" is the default behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7404 ../../ref-manual/varlocality.rst:166
msgid ":term:`PACKAGES`"
msgstr ":term:`PACKAGES`"

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7406
msgid ""
"The list of packages the recipe creates. The default value is the "
"following::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7411
msgid ""
"During packaging, the :ref:`ref-tasks-package` task goes through "
":term:`PACKAGES` and uses the :term:`FILES` variable corresponding to "
"each package to assign files to the package. If a file matches the "
":term:`FILES` variable for more than one package in :term:`PACKAGES`, it "
"will be assigned to the earliest (leftmost) package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7418
msgid ""
"Packages in the variable's list that are empty (i.e. where none of the "
"patterns in ``FILES:``\\ pkg match any files installed by the :ref:`ref-"
"tasks-install` task) are not generated, unless generation is forced "
"through the :term:`ALLOW_EMPTY` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7423
msgid ":term:`PACKAGES_DYNAMIC`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7425
msgid ""
"A promise that your recipe satisfies runtime dependencies for optional "
"modules that are found in other recipes. :term:`PACKAGES_DYNAMIC` does "
"not actually satisfy the dependencies, it only states that they should be"
" satisfied. For example, if a hard, runtime dependency (:term:`RDEPENDS`)"
" of another package is satisfied at build time through the "
":term:`PACKAGES_DYNAMIC` variable, but a package with the module name is "
"never actually produced, then the other package will be broken. Thus, if "
"you attempt to include that package in an image, you will get a "
"dependency failure from the packaging system during the :ref:`ref-tasks-"
"rootfs` task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7437
msgid ""
"Typically, if there is a chance that such a situation can occur and the "
"package that is not created is valid without the dependency being "
"satisfied, then you should use :term:`RRECOMMENDS` (a soft runtime "
"dependency) instead of :term:`RDEPENDS`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7442
msgid ""
"For an example of how to use the :term:`PACKAGES_DYNAMIC` variable when "
"you are splitting packages, see the \":ref:`dev-manual/packages:handling "
"optional module packaging`\" section in the Yocto Project Development "
"Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7446
msgid ":term:`PACKAGESPLITFUNCS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7448
msgid ""
"Specifies a list of functions run to perform additional splitting of "
"files into individual packages. Recipes can either prepend to this "
"variable or prepend to the ``populate_packages`` function in order to "
"perform additional package splitting. In either case, the function should"
" set :term:`PACKAGES`, :term:`FILES`, :term:`RDEPENDS` and other "
"packaging variables appropriately in order to perform the desired "
"splitting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7459
msgid ""
"Extra options passed to the build tool command (``make``, ``ninja`` or "
"more specific build engines, like the Go language one) during the :ref"
":`ref-tasks-compile` task, to specify parallel compilation on the local "
"build host. This variable is usually in the form \"-j x\", where x "
"represents the maximum number of parallel threads such engines can run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7468
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"For software compiled by ``make``, in order for :term:`PARALLEL_MAKE` to "
"be effective, ``make`` must be called with ``${``\\ "
":term:`EXTRA_OEMAKE`\\ ``}``. An easy way to ensure this is to use the "
"``oe_runmake`` function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7473
msgid ""
"By default, the OpenEmbedded build system automatically sets this "
"variable to be equal to the number of cores the build system uses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7478
msgid ""
"If the software being built experiences dependency issues during the :ref"
":`ref-tasks-compile` task that result in race conditions, you can clear "
"the :term:`PARALLEL_MAKE` variable within the recipe as a workaround. For"
" information on addressing race conditions, see the \":ref:`dev-"
"manual/debugging:debugging parallel make races`\" section in the Yocto "
"Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7485
msgid ""
"For single socket systems (i.e. one CPU), you should not have to override"
" this variable to gain optimal parallelism during builds. However, if you"
" have very large systems that employ multiple physical CPUs, you might "
"want to make sure the :term:`PARALLEL_MAKE` variable is not set higher "
"than \"-j 20\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7498
msgid ":term:`PARALLEL_MAKEINST`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7500
msgid ""
"Extra options passed to the build tool install command (``make install``,"
" ``ninja install`` or more specific ones) during the :ref:`ref-tasks-"
"install` task in order to specify parallel installation. This variable "
"defaults to the value of :term:`PARALLEL_MAKE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7508
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"For software compiled by ``make``, in order for :term:`PARALLEL_MAKEINST`"
" to be effective, ``make`` must be called with ``${``\\ "
":term:`EXTRA_OEMAKE`\\ ``}``. An easy way to ensure this is to use the "
"``oe_runmake`` function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7513
msgid ""
"If the software being built experiences dependency issues during the :ref"
":`ref-tasks-install` task that result in race conditions, you can clear "
"the :term:`PARALLEL_MAKEINST` variable within the recipe as a workaround."
" For information on addressing race conditions, see the \":ref:`dev-"
"manual/debugging:debugging parallel make races`\" section in the Yocto "
"Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7519
msgid ":term:`PATCHRESOLVE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7521
msgid ""
"Determines the action to take when a patch fails. You can set this "
"variable to one of two values: \"noop\" and \"user\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7524
msgid ""
"The default value of \"noop\" causes the build to simply fail when the "
"OpenEmbedded build system cannot successfully apply a patch. Setting the "
"value to \"user\" causes the build system to launch a shell and places "
"you in the right location so that you can manually resolve the conflicts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7530
msgid "Set this variable in your :ref:`structure-build-conf-local.conf` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7531
msgid ":term:`PATCHTOOL`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7533
msgid ""
"Specifies the utility used to apply patches for a recipe during the :ref"
":`ref-tasks-patch` task. You can specify one of three utilities: "
"\"patch\", \"quilt\", or \"git\". The default utility used is \"quilt\" "
"except for the quilt-native recipe itself. Because the quilt tool is not "
"available at the time quilt-native is being patched, it uses \"patch\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7540
msgid ""
"If you wish to use an alternative patching tool, set the variable in the "
"recipe using one of the following::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7546
msgid ":term:`PE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7548
msgid ""
"The epoch of the recipe. By default, this variable is unset. The variable"
" is used to make upgrades possible when the versioning scheme changes in "
"some backwards incompatible way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7552
msgid ":term:`PE` is the default value of the :term:`PKGE` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7553
msgid ":term:`PEP517_WHEEL_PATH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7555
msgid ""
"When used by recipes that inherit the :ref:`ref-classes-python_pep517` "
"class, denotes the path to ``dist/`` (short for distribution) where the "
"binary archive ``wheel`` is built."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7558
msgid ":term:`PERSISTENT_DIR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7560
msgid "See :term:`bitbake:PERSISTENT_DIR` in the BitBake manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7561
msgid ":term:`PF`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7563
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Specifies the recipe or package name and includes all version and "
"revision numbers (i.e. ``glibc-2.13-r20+svnr15508/`` and "
"``bash-4.2-r1/``). This variable is comprised of the following: "
"${:term:`PN`}-${:term:`EXTENDPE`}${:term:`PV`}-${:term:`PR`}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7567
msgid ":term:`PIXBUF_PACKAGES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7569
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"When inheriting the :ref:`ref-classes-pixbufcache` class, this variable "
"identifies packages that contain the pixbuf loaders used with ``gdk-"
"pixbuf``. By default, the :ref:`ref-classes-pixbufcache` class assumes "
"that the loaders are in the recipe's main package (i.e. ``${``\\ "
":term:`PN`\\ ``}``). Use this variable if the loaders you need are in a "
"package other than that main package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7576
msgid ":term:`PKG`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7578
msgid ""
"The name of the resulting package created by the OpenEmbedded build "
"system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7583
msgid "When using the :term:`PKG` variable, you must use a package name override."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7585
msgid ""
"For example, when the :ref:`ref-classes-debian` class renames the output "
"package, it does so by setting ``PKG:packagename``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7587
msgid ":term:`PKG_CONFIG_PATH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7589
msgid ""
"The path to ``pkg-config`` files for the current build context. ``pkg-"
"config`` reads this variable from the environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7591
msgid ":term:`PKGD`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7593
msgid ""
"Points to the destination directory for files to be packaged before they "
"are split into individual packages. This directory defaults to the "
"following::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7599 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7635
msgid "Do not change this default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7600
msgid ":term:`PKGDATA_DIR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7602
msgid ""
"Points to a shared, global-state directory that holds data generated "
"during the packaging process. During the packaging process, the :ref"
":`ref-tasks-packagedata` task packages data for each recipe and installs "
"it into this shared area. This directory defaults to the following, which"
" you should not change::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7611
msgid ""
"For examples of how this data is used, see the \":ref:`overview-"
"manual/concepts:automatically added runtime dependencies`\" section in "
"the Yocto Project Overview and Concepts Manual and the \":ref:`dev-"
"manual/debugging:viewing package information with ``oe-pkgdata-util```\" "
"section in the Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7616
msgid ":term:`PKGDEST`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7618
msgid ""
"Points to the parent directory for files to be packaged after they have "
"been split into individual packages. This directory defaults to the "
"following::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7624
msgid ""
"Under this directory, the build system creates directories for each "
"package specified in :term:`PACKAGES`. Do not change this default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7627
msgid ":term:`PKGDESTWORK`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7629
msgid ""
"Points to a temporary work area where the :ref:`ref-tasks-package` task "
"saves package metadata. The :term:`PKGDESTWORK` location defaults to the "
"following::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7637
msgid ""
"The :ref:`ref-tasks-packagedata` task copies the package metadata from "
":term:`PKGDESTWORK` to :term:`PKGDATA_DIR` to make it available globally."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7640
msgid ":term:`PKGE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7642
msgid ""
"The epoch of the package(s) built by the recipe. By default, :term:`PKGE`"
" is set to :term:`PE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7644
msgid ":term:`PKGR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7646
msgid ""
"The revision of the package(s) built by the recipe. By default, "
":term:`PKGR` is set to :term:`PR`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7648
msgid ":term:`PKGV`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7650
msgid ""
"The version of the package(s) built by the recipe. By default, "
":term:`PKGV` is set to :term:`PV`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7653
msgid ""
"If :term:`PV` contains the ``+`` sign, source control information will be"
" included in :term:`PKGV` later in the packaging phase. For more "
"information, see the :doc:`/dev-manual/external-scm` section of the Yocto"
" Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7660
msgid ""
"Since source control information is included in a late stage by the :ref"
":`ref-classes-package` class, it cannot be seen from the BitBake "
"environment with ``bitbake -e`` or ``bitbake-getvar``. Instead, after the"
" package is built, the version information can be retrieved with ``oe-"
"pkgdata-util package-info <package name>``. See the :ref:`dev-"
"manual/debugging:Viewing Package Information with ``oe-pkgdata-util``` "
"section of the Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual for more "
"information on ``oe-pkgdata-util``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7669
msgid ":term:`PN`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7671
msgid ""
"This variable can have two separate functions depending on the context: a"
" recipe name or a resulting package name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7674
msgid ""
":term:`PN` refers to a recipe name in the context of a file used by the "
"OpenEmbedded build system as input to create a package. The name is "
"normally extracted from the recipe file name. For example, if the recipe "
"is named ``expat_2.0.1.bb``, then the default value of :term:`PN` will be"
" \"expat\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7680
msgid ""
"The variable refers to a package name in the context of a file created or"
" produced by the OpenEmbedded build system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7683
msgid ""
"If applicable, the :term:`PN` variable also contains any special suffix "
"or prefix. For example, using ``bash`` to build packages for the native "
"machine, :term:`PN` is ``bash-native``. Using ``bash`` to build packages "
"for the target and for Multilib, :term:`PN` would be ``bash`` and "
"``lib64-bash``, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7688
msgid ":term:`POPULATE_SDK_POST_HOST_COMMAND`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7690
msgid ""
"Specifies a list of functions to call once the OpenEmbedded build system "
"has created the host part of the SDK. You can specify functions separated"
" by spaces::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7696 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7708
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"If you need to pass the SDK path to a command within a function, you can "
"use ``${SDK_DIR}``, which points to the parent directory used by the "
"OpenEmbedded build system when creating SDK output. See the "
":term:`SDK_DIR` variable for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7700
msgid ":term:`POPULATE_SDK_POST_TARGET_COMMAND`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7702
msgid ""
"Specifies a list of functions to call once the OpenEmbedded build system "
"has created the target part of the SDK. You can specify functions "
"separated by spaces::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7712
msgid ":term:`PR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7714
msgid ""
"The revision of the recipe. The default value for this variable is "
"\"r0\". Subsequent revisions of the recipe conventionally have the values"
" \"r1\", \"r2\", and so forth. When :term:`PV` increases, :term:`PR` is "
"conventionally reset to \"r0\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7721
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system does not need the aid of :term:`PR` to know"
" when to rebuild a recipe. The build system uses the task :ref:`input "
"checksums <overview-manual/concepts:checksums (signatures)>` along with "
"the :ref:`stamp <structure-build-tmp-stamps>` and :ref:`overview-"
"manual/concepts:shared state cache` mechanisms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7728
msgid ""
"The :term:`PR` variable primarily becomes significant when a package "
"manager dynamically installs packages on an already built image. In this "
"case, :term:`PR`, which is the default value of :term:`PKGR`, helps the "
"package manager distinguish which package is the most recent one in cases"
" where many packages have the same :term:`PV` (i.e. :term:`PKGV`). A "
"component having many packages with the same :term:`PV` usually means "
"that the packages all install the same upstream version, but with later "
"(:term:`PR`) version packages including packaging fixes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7740
msgid ""
":term:`PR` does not need to be increased for changes that do not change "
"the package contents or metadata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7743
msgid ""
"Because manually managing :term:`PR` can be cumbersome and error-prone, "
"an automated solution exists. See the \":ref:`dev-manual/packages:working"
" with a pr service`\" section in the Yocto Project Development Tasks "
"Manual for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7747
msgid ":term:`PREFERRED_PROVIDER`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7749
msgid ""
"If multiple recipes provide the same item, this variable determines which"
" recipe is preferred and thus provides the item (i.e. the preferred "
"provider). You should always suffix this variable with the name of the "
"provided item. And, you should define the variable using the preferred "
"recipe's name (:term:`PN`). Here is a common example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7758
msgid ""
"In the previous example, multiple recipes are providing "
"\"virtual/kernel\". The :term:`PREFERRED_PROVIDER` variable is set with "
"the name (:term:`PN`) of the recipe you prefer to provide "
"\"virtual/kernel\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7762
msgid "Here are more examples::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7767
msgid ""
"For more information, see the \":ref:`dev-manual/new-recipe:using virtual"
" providers`\" section in the Yocto Project Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7773
msgid ""
"If you use a ``virtual/\\*`` item with :term:`PREFERRED_PROVIDER`, then "
"any recipe that :term:`PROVIDES` that item but is not selected (defined) "
"by :term:`PREFERRED_PROVIDER` is prevented from building, which is "
"usually desirable since this mechanism is designed to select between "
"mutually exclusive alternative providers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7778
msgid ":term:`PREFERRED_PROVIDERS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7780
msgid "See :term:`bitbake:PREFERRED_PROVIDERS` in the BitBake manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7781
msgid ":term:`PREFERRED_RPROVIDER`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7783
msgid ""
"The :term:`PREFERRED_RPROVIDER` variable works like the "
":term:`PREFERRED_PROVIDER` variable, but it denotes packages that provide"
" a *runtime* component. Runtime providers are declared in recipes that "
"set the :term:`RPROVIDES` variable for a specific package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7792
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"This statement sets the runtime provider for the X terminal emulator to "
"``rxvt-unicode``. The ``rxvt-unicode`` package is a runtime provider of "
"this component because the ``rxvt-unicode`` recipe set the following "
":term:`RPROVIDES` definition for the ``rxvt-unicode`` (``${PN}``) "
"package::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7800
msgid ""
"For more information on virtual providers, see the \":ref:`dev-manual"
"/new-recipe:using virtual providers`\" section in the Yocto Project "
"Development Tasks Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7803
msgid ":term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7805
msgid ""
"The :term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN` variable selects the toolchain to use for"
" compiling recipes. This variable is not meant to be overridden globally."
" Instead, the values of :term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_TARGET`, "
":term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_NATIVE` and :term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_SDK` "
"should be overridden."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7810
msgid ":term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_NATIVE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7812
msgid ""
"This variable controls the toolchain used for compiling :ref:`ref-"
"classes-native` recipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7815 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7828
msgid "This variable should be set globally from a :term:`configuration file`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7817 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7830
msgid ""
"See :term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_TARGET` for more details on the possible "
"values for this variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7820
msgid ""
"A recipe that does not support the toolchain specified by "
":term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_NATIVE` can override it locally with "
":term:`TOOLCHAIN_NATIVE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7823
msgid ":term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_SDK`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7825
msgid ""
"This variable controls the toolchain used for compiling :ref:`ref-"
"classes-nativesdk` recipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7832
msgid ":term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_TARGET`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7834
msgid ""
"This variable controls the toolchain used for compiling recipes in the "
"architecture of the target :term:`MACHINE`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7837
msgid "There are two possible values for this variable at the moment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7839
msgid ""
":ref:`gcc <ref-classes-toolchain-gcc>` (default): the GCC/Binutils "
"toolchain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7840
msgid ":ref:`clang <ref-classes-toolchain-clang>`: the Clang/LLVM toolchain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7842
msgid ""
":term:`PREFERRED_TOOLCHAIN_TARGET` will make the :ref:`ref-classes-base` "
"class inherit one of the toolchain classes defined in "
":oe_git:`meta/classes/toolchain </openembedded-"
"core/tree/meta/classes/toolchain>`. As a consequence, this variable "
"should be set globally from a :term:`configuration file`."
msgstr ""

