#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:7999
msgid ""
"In addition to providing recipes under alternate names, the "
":term:`PROVIDES` mechanism is also used to implement virtual targets. A "
"virtual target is a name that corresponds to some particular "
"functionality (e.g. a Linux kernel). Recipes that provide the "
"functionality in question list the virtual target in :term:`PROVIDES`. "
"Recipes that depend on the functionality in question can include the "
"virtual target in :term:`DEPENDS` to leave the choice of provider open."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:8007
msgid ""
"Conventionally, virtual targets have names on the form "
"\"virtual/function\" (e.g. \"virtual/kernel\"). The slash is simply part "
"of the name and has no syntactical significance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:8011
msgid ""
"The :term:`PREFERRED_PROVIDER` variable is used to select which "
"particular recipe provides a virtual target."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:8016
msgid ""
"A corresponding mechanism for virtual runtime dependencies (packages) "
"exists. However, the mechanism does not depend on any special "
"functionality beyond ordinary variable assignments. For example, :term"
":`VIRTUAL-RUNTIME_dev_manager <VIRTUAL-RUNTIME>` refers to the package of"
" the component that manages the ``/dev`` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:8022
msgid ""
"Setting the \"preferred provider\" for runtime dependencies is as simple "
"as using the following assignment in a configuration file::"
