"value of :term:`SYSTEMD_PACKAGES`. Overrides not included in "
":term:`SYSTEMD_PACKAGES` will be silently ignored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10783
msgid ":term:`SYSVINIT_ENABLED_GETTYS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10785
msgid ""
"When using :ref:`SysVinit <dev-manual/new-recipe:enabling system "
"services>`, specifies a space-separated list of the virtual terminals "
"that should run a :wikipedia:`getty <Getty_(Unix)>` (allowing login), "
"assuming :term:`USE_VT` is not set to \"0\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10790
msgid ""
"The default value for :term:`SYSVINIT_ENABLED_GETTYS` is \"1\" (i.e. only"
" run a getty on the first virtual terminal)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10792
msgid ":term:`T`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10794
msgid ""
"This variable points to a directory were BitBake places temporary files, "
"which consist mostly of task logs and scripts, when building a particular"
" recipe. The variable is typically set as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10800
msgid ""
"The :term:`WORKDIR` is the directory into which BitBake unpacks and "
"builds the recipe. The default ``bitbake.conf`` file sets this variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10804
msgid ""
"The :term:`T` variable is not to be confused with the :term:`TMPDIR` "
"variable, which points to the root of the directory tree where BitBake "
"places the output of an entire build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10808 ../../ref-manual/varlocality.rst:53
msgid ":term:`TARGET_ARCH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10810
msgid ""
"The target machine's architecture. The OpenEmbedded build system supports"
" many architectures. Here is an example list of architectures supported. "
"This list is by no means complete as the architecture is configurable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10823
msgid ""
"For additional information on machine architectures, see the "
":term:`TUNE_ARCH` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10825
msgid ":term:`TARGET_AS_ARCH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10827
msgid ""
"Specifies architecture-specific assembler flags for the target system. "
":term:`TARGET_AS_ARCH` is initialized from :term:`TUNE_ASARGS` by default"
" in the BitBake configuration file (``meta/conf/bitbake.conf``)::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10833
msgid ":term:`TARGET_CC_ARCH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10835
msgid ""
"Specifies architecture-specific C compiler flags for the target system. "
":term:`TARGET_CC_ARCH` is initialized from :term:`TUNE_CCARGS` by "
"default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10841
msgid ""
"It is a common workaround to append :term:`LDFLAGS` to "
":term:`TARGET_CC_ARCH` in recipes that build software for the target that"
" would not otherwise respect the exported :term:`LDFLAGS` variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10844
msgid ":term:`TARGET_CC_KERNEL_ARCH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10846
msgid ""
"This is a specific kernel compiler flag for a CPU or Application Binary "
"Interface (ABI) tune. The flag is used rarely and only for cases where a "
"userspace :term:`TUNE_CCARGS` is not compatible with the kernel "
"compilation. The :term:`TARGET_CC_KERNEL_ARCH` variable allows the kernel"
" (and associated modules) to use a different configuration. See the "
"``meta/conf/machine/include/arm/feature-arm-thumb.inc`` file in :term"
":`OpenEmbedded-Core (OE-Core)` for an example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10854
msgid ":term:`TARGET_CFLAGS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10856
msgid ""
"Specifies the flags to pass to the C compiler when building for the "
"target. When building in the target context, :term:`CFLAGS` is set to the"
" value of this variable by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10861
msgid ""
"Additionally, the SDK's environment setup script sets the :term:`CFLAGS` "
"variable in the environment to the :term:`TARGET_CFLAGS` value so that "
"executables built using the SDK also have the flags applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10864
msgid ":term:`TARGET_CPPFLAGS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10866
msgid ""
"Specifies the flags to pass to the C pre-processor (i.e. to both the C "
"and the C++ compilers) when building for the target. When building in the"
" target context, :term:`CPPFLAGS` is set to the value of this variable by"
" default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10871
msgid ""
"Additionally, the SDK's environment setup script sets the "
":term:`CPPFLAGS` variable in the environment to the "
":term:`TARGET_CPPFLAGS` value so that executables built using the SDK "
"also have the flags applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10875
msgid ":term:`TARGET_CXXFLAGS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10877
msgid ""
"Specifies the flags to pass to the C++ compiler when building for the "
"target. When building in the target context, :term:`CXXFLAGS` is set to "
"the value of this variable by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10882
msgid ""
"Additionally, the SDK's environment setup script sets the "
":term:`CXXFLAGS` variable in the environment to the "
":term:`TARGET_CXXFLAGS` value so that executables built using the SDK "
"also have the flags applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10886
msgid ":term:`TARGET_DBGSRC_DIR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10888
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Specifies the target path to debug source files. The default is "
"``/usr/src/debug/${PN}/${PV}``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10890 ../../ref-manual/varlocality.rst:40
msgid ":term:`TARGET_FPU`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10892
msgid ""
"Specifies the method for handling FPU code. For FPU-less targets, which "
"include most ARM CPUs, the variable must be set to \"soft\". If not, the "
"kernel emulation gets used, which results in a performance penalty."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10896
msgid ":term:`TARGET_LD_ARCH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10898
msgid ""
"Specifies architecture-specific linker flags for the target system. "
":term:`TARGET_LD_ARCH` is initialized from :term:`TUNE_LDARGS` by default"
" in the BitBake configuration file (``meta/conf/bitbake.conf``)::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10904
msgid ":term:`TARGET_LDFLAGS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10906
msgid ""
"Specifies the flags to pass to the linker when building for the target. "
"When building in the target context, :term:`LDFLAGS` is set to the value "
"of this variable by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10911
msgid ""
"Additionally, the SDK's environment setup script sets the :term:`LDFLAGS`"
" variable in the environment to the :term:`TARGET_LDFLAGS` value so that "
"executables built using the SDK also have the flags applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10915 ../../ref-manual/varlocality.rst:38
msgid ":term:`TARGET_OS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10917
msgid ""
"Specifies the target's operating system. The variable can be set to "
"\"linux\" for glibc-based systems (GNU C Library) and to \"linux-musl\" "
"for musl libc. For ARM/EABI targets, the possible values are \"linux-"
"gnueabi\" and \"linux-musleabi\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10921
msgid ":term:`TARGET_PREFIX`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10923
msgid "Specifies the prefix used for the toolchain binary target tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10925
msgid ""
"Depending on the type of recipe and the build target, "
":term:`TARGET_PREFIX` is set as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10928
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"For recipes building for the target machine, the value is "
"\"${:term:`TARGET_SYS`}-\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10931
msgid ""
"For native recipes, the build system sets the variable to the value of "
":term:`BUILD_PREFIX`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10934
msgid ""
"For native SDK recipes (:ref:`ref-classes-nativesdk`), the build system "
"sets the variable to the value of :term:`SDK_PREFIX`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10936
msgid ":term:`TARGET_SYS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10942
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system automatically sets this variable based on "
":term:`TARGET_ARCH`, :term:`TARGET_VENDOR`, and :term:`TARGET_OS` "
"variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10949
msgid "You do not need to set the :term:`TARGET_SYS` variable yourself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10953
msgid ""
"Given a native recipe on a 32-bit, x86 machine running Linux, the value "
"is \"i686-linux\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10956
msgid ""
"Given a recipe being built for a little-endian, MIPS target running "
"Linux, the value might be \"mipsel-linux\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10958
msgid ":term:`TARGET_VENDOR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10960
msgid "Specifies the name of the target vendor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10961
msgid ":term:`TC_CXX_RUNTIME`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10963
msgid ""
"Specifies the C/C++ STL and runtime variant to use during the build "
"process. Default value is 'gnu'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10966
msgid "You can select \"gnu\", \"llvm\", or \"android\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10967 ../../ref-manual/varlocality.rst:44
msgid ":term:`TCLIBC`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10969
msgid ""
"Specifies the GNU standard C library (``libc``) variant to use during the"
" build process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10972
msgid "You can select \"glibc\", \"musl\", \"newlib\", or \"baremetal\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10973 ../../ref-manual/varlocality.rst:42
msgid ":term:`TCMODE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10975
msgid ""
"Specifies the toolchain selector. :term:`TCMODE` controls the "
"characteristics of the generated packages and images by telling the "
"OpenEmbedded build system which toolchain profile to use. By default, the"
" OpenEmbedded build system builds its own internal toolchain. The "
"variable's default value is \"default\", which uses that internal "
"toolchain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10984
msgid ""
"If :term:`TCMODE` is set to a value other than \"default\", then it is "
"your responsibility to ensure that the toolchain is compatible with the "
"default toolchain. Using older or newer versions of these components "
"might cause build problems. See :doc:`Release Information </migration-"
"guides/index>` for your version of the Yocto Project, to find the "
"specific components with which the toolchain must be compatible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10992
msgid ""
"The :term:`TCMODE` variable is similar to :term:`TCLIBC`, which controls "
"the variant of the GNU standard C library (``libc``) used during the "
"build process: ``glibc`` or ``musl``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:10996
msgid ""
"With additional layers, it is possible to use a pre-compiled external "
"toolchain. One example is the Sourcery G++ Toolchain. The support for "
"this toolchain resides in the separate Mentor Graphics ``meta-sourcery`` "
"layer at https://github.com/MentorEmbedded/meta-sourcery/."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11002
msgid ""
"The layer's ``README`` file contains information on how to use the "
"Sourcery G++ Toolchain as an external toolchain. You will have to add the"
" layer to your :ref:`structure-build-conf-bblayers.conf` file and then "
"set the :term:`EXTERNAL_TOOLCHAIN` variable in your :ref:`structure-"
"build-conf-local.conf` file to the location of the toolchain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11008
msgid ""
"The fundamentals used for this example apply to any external toolchain. "
"You can use ``meta-sourcery`` as a template for adding support for other "
"external toolchains."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11012
msgid ""
"In addition to toolchain configuration, you will also need a "
"corresponding toolchain recipe file. This recipe file needs to package up"
" any pre-built objects in the toolchain such as ``libgcc``, "
"``libstdcc++``, any locales, and ``libc``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11016
msgid ":term:`TEMPLATECONF`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11018
msgid ""
"Specifies the directory used by the build system to find templates from "
"which to build the :ref:`structure-build-conf-bblayers.conf` and :ref"
":`structure-build-conf-local.conf` files. Use this variable if you wish "
"to customize such files, and the default BitBake targets shown when "
"sourcing the :ref:`structure-core-script` script."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11024
msgid ""
"For details, see the :ref:`dev-manual/custom-template-configuration-"
"directory:creating a custom template configuration directory` section in "
"the Yocto Project Development Tasks manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11030
msgid ""
"You must set this variable in the external environment in order for it to"
" work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11032
msgid ":term:`TEST_EXPORT_DIR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11034
msgid ""
"The location the OpenEmbedded build system uses to export tests when the "
":term:`TEST_EXPORT_ONLY` variable is set to \"1\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11038
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The :term:`TEST_EXPORT_DIR` variable defaults to "
"``\"${TMPDIR}/testimage/${PN}\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11040
msgid ":term:`TEST_EXPORT_ONLY`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11042
msgid ""
"Specifies to export the tests only. Set this variable to \"1\" if you do "
"not want to run the tests but you want them to be exported in a manner "
"that you to run them outside of the build system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11045
msgid ":term:`TEST_LOG_DIR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11047
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Holds the SSH log and the boot log for QEMU machines. The "
":term:`TEST_LOG_DIR` variable defaults to ``\"${WORKDIR}/testimage\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11052
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Actual test results reside in the task log (``log.do_testimage``), which "
"is in the ``${WORKDIR}/temp/`` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11054
msgid ":term:`TEST_POWERCONTROL_CMD`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11056
msgid ""
"For automated hardware testing, specifies the command to use to control "
"the power of the target machine under test. Typically, this command would"
" point to a script that performs the appropriate action (e.g. interacting"
" with a web-enabled power strip). The specified command should expect to "
"receive as the last argument \"off\", \"on\" or \"cycle\" specifying to "
"power off, on, or cycle (power off and then power on) the device, "
"respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11063
msgid ":term:`TEST_POWERCONTROL_EXTRA_ARGS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11065
msgid ""
"For automated hardware testing, specifies additional arguments to pass "
"through to the command specified in :term:`TEST_POWERCONTROL_CMD`. "
"Setting :term:`TEST_POWERCONTROL_EXTRA_ARGS` is optional. You can use it "
"if you wish, for example, to separate the machine-specific and non-"
"machine-specific parts of the arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11071
msgid ":term:`TEST_QEMUBOOT_TIMEOUT`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11073
msgid ""
"The time in seconds allowed for an image to boot before automated runtime"
" tests begin to run against an image. The default timeout period to allow"
" the boot process to reach the login prompt is 500 seconds. You can "
"specify a different value in a :term:`configuration file`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11078 ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11161
msgid ""
"For more information on testing images, see the \":ref:`test-manual"
"/runtime-testing:performing automated runtime testing`\" section in the "
"Yocto Project Test Environment Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11081
msgid ":term:`TEST_SERIALCONTROL_CMD`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11083
msgid ""
"For automated hardware testing, specifies the command to use to connect "
"to the serial console of the target machine under test. This command "
"simply needs to connect to the serial console and forward that connection"
" to standard input and output as any normal terminal program does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11089
msgid ""
"For example, to use the Picocom terminal program on serial device "
"``/dev/ttyUSB0`` at 115200bps, you would set the variable as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11093
msgid ":term:`TEST_SERIALCONTROL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11095
msgid ""
"For automated hardware testing with the :ref:`ref-classes-testexport` "
"class, the :term:`TEST_SERIALCONTROL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT` variable specifies "
"the timeout in seconds for the initial connection to the target."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11098
msgid ":term:`TEST_SERIALCONTROL_EXTRA_ARGS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11100
msgid ""
"For automated hardware testing, specifies additional arguments to pass "
"through to the command specified in :term:`TEST_SERIALCONTROL_CMD`. "
"Setting :term:`TEST_SERIALCONTROL_EXTRA_ARGS` is optional. You can use it"
" if you wish, for example, to separate the machine-specific and non-"
"machine-specific parts of the command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11106
msgid ":term:`TEST_SERIALCONTROL_PS1`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11108
msgid ""
"For automated hardware testing with the :ref:`ref-classes-testexport` "
"class, the :term:`TEST_SERIALCONTROL_PS1` variable specifies a regex "
"string representing an empty prompt on the target terminal. For example: "
"``root@target:.*#``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11112
msgid ":term:`TEST_SERVER_IP`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11114
msgid ""
"The IP address of the build machine (host machine). This IP address is "
"usually automatically detected. However, if detection fails, this "
"variable needs to be set to the IP address of the build machine (i.e. "
"where the build is taking place)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11121
msgid ""
"The :term:`TEST_SERVER_IP` variable is only used for a small number of "
"tests such as the \"dnf\" test suite, which needs to download packages "
"from ``WORKDIR/oe-rootfs-repo``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11124
msgid ":term:`TEST_SUITES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11126
msgid ""
"An ordered list of tests (modules) to run against an image when "
"performing automated runtime testing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11129
msgid ""
"The OpenEmbedded build system provides a core set of tests that can be "
"used against images."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11134
msgid "Currently, there is only support for running these tests under QEMU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11137
msgid ""
"Tests include ``ping``, ``ssh``, ``df`` among others. You can add your "
"own tests to the list of tests by appending :term:`TEST_SUITES` as "
"follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11143
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can provide the \"auto\" option to have all applicable"
" tests run against the image::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11149
msgid ""
"Using this option causes the build system to automatically run tests that"
" are applicable to the image. Tests that are not applicable are skipped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11153
msgid ""
"The order in which tests are run is important. Tests that depend on "
"another test must appear later in the list than the test on which they "
"depend. For example, if you append the list of tests with two tests "
"(``test_A`` and ``test_B``) where ``test_B`` is dependent on ``test_A``, "
"then you must order the tests as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11164
msgid ":term:`TEST_TARGET`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11166
msgid ""
"Specifies the target controller to use when running tests against a test "
"image. The default controller to use is \"qemu\"::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11171
msgid ""
"A target controller is a class that defines how an image gets deployed on"
" a target and how a target is started. A layer can extend the controllers"
" by adding a module in the layer's ``/lib/oeqa/controllers`` directory "
"and by inheriting the ``BaseTarget`` class, which is an abstract class "
"that cannot be used as a value of :term:`TEST_TARGET`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11178
msgid "You can provide the following arguments with :term:`TEST_TARGET`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11180
msgid ""
"*\"qemu\":* Boots a QEMU image and runs the tests. See the \":ref:`test-"
"manual/runtime-testing:enabling runtime tests on qemu`\" section in the "
"Yocto Project Test Environment Manual for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11185
msgid ""
"*\"simpleremote\":* Runs the tests on target hardware that is already up "
"and running. The hardware can be on the network or it can be a device "
"running an image on QEMU. You must also set :term:`TEST_TARGET_IP` when "
"you use \"simpleremote\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11193
msgid "This argument is defined in ``meta/lib/oeqa/controllers/simpleremote.py``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11196
msgid ""
"For information on running tests on hardware, see the \":ref:`test-manual"
"/runtime-testing:enabling runtime tests on hardware`\" section in the "
"Yocto Project Test Environment Manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11199
msgid ":term:`TEST_TARGET_IP`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11201
msgid ""
"The IP address of your hardware under test. The :term:`TEST_TARGET_IP` "
"variable has no effect when :term:`TEST_TARGET` is set to \"qemu\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11205
msgid ""
"When you specify the IP address, you can also include a port. Here is an "
"example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11210
msgid ""
"Specifying a port is useful when SSH is started on a non-standard port or"
" in cases when your hardware under test is behind a firewall or network "
"that is not directly accessible from your host and you need to do port "
"address translation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11215
msgid ":term:`TESTIMAGE_AUTO`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11217
msgid ""
"Automatically runs the series of automated tests for images when an image"
" is successfully built. Setting :term:`TESTIMAGE_AUTO` to \"1\" causes "
"any image that successfully builds to automatically boot under QEMU. "
"Using the variable also adds in dependencies so that any SDK for which "
"testing is requested is automatically built first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11223
msgid ""
"These tests are written in Python making use of the ``unittest`` module, "
"and the majority of them run commands on the target system over ``ssh``. "
"You can set this variable to \"1\" in a :term:`configuration file` to "
"have the OpenEmbedded build system automatically run these tests after an"
" image successfully builds:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11229
msgid "TESTIMAGE_AUTO = \"1\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11231
msgid ""
"For more information on enabling, running, and writing these tests, see "
"the \":ref:`test-manual/runtime-testing:performing automated runtime "
"testing`\" section in the Yocto Project Test Environment Manual and the "
"\":ref:`ref-classes-testimage`\" section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11236
msgid ":term:`TESTIMAGE_FAILED_QA_ARTIFACTS`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11238
msgid ""
"When using the :ref:`ref-classes-testimage` class, the variable "
":term:`TESTIMAGE_FAILED_QA_ARTIFACTS`  lists space-separated paths on the"
" target to retrieve onto the host."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11241
msgid ":term:`TESTSDK_SUITES`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11243
msgid ""
"When using the :ref:`ref-classes-testsdk` class, the "
":term:`TESTSDK_SUITES` variable can be used to control the list of test "
"modules to run. The default value for this variable is an empty string "
"and all test modules that can be found are run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11247
msgid ":term:`THISDIR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11249
msgid ""
"The directory in which the file BitBake is currently parsing is located. "
"Do not manually set this variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11251
msgid ":term:`TIME`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11253
msgid ""
"The time the build was started. Times appear using the hour, minute, and "
"second (HMS) format (e.g. \"140159\" for one minute and fifty-nine "
"seconds past 1400 hours)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11256
msgid ":term:`TMPDIR`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11258
msgid ""
"This variable is the base directory the OpenEmbedded build system uses "
"for all build output and intermediate files (other than the shared state "
"cache). By default, the :term:`TMPDIR` variable points to ``tmp`` within "
"the :term:`Build Directory`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11263
msgid ""
"If you want to establish this directory in a location other than the "
"default, you can set it to another value in your :ref:`structure-build-"
"conf-site.conf` configuration file::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11269
msgid ""
"An example use for this scenario is to set :term:`TMPDIR` to a local "
"disk, which does not use NFS, while having the :term:`Build Directory` "
"use NFS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../ref-manual/variables.rst:11272
msgid ""
"The filesystem used by :term:`TMPDIR` must have standard filesystem "
"semantics (i.e. mixed-case files are unique, POSIX file locking, and "
"persistent inodes). Due to various issues with NFS and bugs in some "
"implementations, NFS does not meet this minimum requirement. "
"Consequently, :term:`TMPDIR` cannot be on NFS."
msgstr ""

